model {

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE MACHINE — one command in, background workers out.
  // Every value here was read off CLI/bin/sf, CLI/src/beads-loop/ (index.sh and the nine beside it),
  // CLI/utils/console.sh, CLI/utils/logging.sh,
  // CLI/utils/forge.sh, CLI/utils/user-env.sh, scripts/clone-webs.sh, CLI/spider-factory.example.env,
  // CLI/src/agent-hooks/worker-stop.sh.
  // The cast only: the story is told by the four walkthroughs in views.c4.
  // Rules for editing these files: ./REFERENCE.md — the code is the only source of truth.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  machine = layer "The machine — one command in, background workers out" {
    summary "One command in, background workers out: the command, the installer, the webs cloner, the checks, the parameters, the dispatcher, the claim."

    sfCli = cliTool "sf — the one command, and its seven" {
      summary "The whole CLI: one bash file, seven commands, each one an engine beside it. Every one but bare `sf` runs only from inside the factory."
      description """
**What it is:** `CLI/bin/sf` — one bash file, no node. The installer puts a launcher for it at
`~/.local/bin/sf`. Every command is an arm of one `case` block, and nothing else is reachable.

| You type | What runs |
|---|---|
| `sf` | an agent session through `SF_AGENT_COMMAND` (default `claude`), **from anywhere, always at the factory root** — already inside it, you stay put; anything you type after it goes to that session |
| `sf --help` / `-h` / `help` | the usage block, from anywhere: the seven commands, the prompt forms, and where every setting lives |
| `sf loop` | `CLI/src/beads-loop/index.sh`, foreground — the dispatcher over the tower in the current directory |
| `sf plan:epic` / `sf plan:feature` / `sf plan:task` | `CLI/src/plan-agent.sh` with that kind, and everything you typed after the command handed to it as the prompt |
| `sf overseer` | `CLI/src/overseer-agent.sh`, foreground — the reader, `sf` denied to it |
| `sf commit <line>` | `CLI/src/commit.sh` — the only thing that commits, anywhere |
| `sf hook stop` | `CLI/src/agent-hooks/worker-stop.sh` — the worker's Stop hook, an engine beside every other one, located by `locateEngines` and execed through the same `requireEngine` check. Nobody types it: each of the seven worker agent files registers it on its own `Stop` event, and it reads its own stdin. A second word dies naming the one |

**Three checks, and where each one lives.** `requireInsideFactory` is the first line of all five command
dispatchers, so it is the first thing every one of the seven does: it matches `$PWD/` against
`<factory root>/*`, the root being **two levels up from the script's own folder** — `locateEngines` resolves
`$0` to `CLI/bin`, walks `$here/../..`, and keeps the result in `$factoryRoot` — so the root itself, or anything nested
under it at any depth, passes; anything else dies naming the root and telling you to `cd` into it, and says
bare `sf` opens a session from anywhere. Bare `sf` and `sf --help` never meet it — bare `sf` answers from
wherever you are and `cd`s to that root itself, and it `cd`s to **the same `$factoryRoot`**, the one
`locateEngines` already computed: the arm derives nothing of its own, because a second computation of the same
path is a second fact that can disagree with the first. `requireEngine` runs next, before every command: the engine
file is actually there, or the message names the missing path and says re-run `CLI/install-cli.sh`.
`requireBeadsProject` — a beads database reachable from here — is **one predicate in `CLI/utils/console.sh`**, not a
copy per command: `sf` runs it only for `loop`, the three planners and the watcher each call that same one inside
their engine, the two id generators call it before they mint anything, and `commit` needs only a git repo.

**Who else stands at that door.** Every `sf` call a worker or a hook makes is a command and so carries the
same requirement: the `sf hook stop` that reaches the hook at all, the worker's `sf commit` as it goes, and the
Stop hook's `sf commit` on its behalf.
They all have it — the loop will only run a tower from inside the factory, and the worker, the hook and the
tower all sit under that same root, the worktree being `<tower>/.claude/worktrees/…`. **That guard in front of
the hook is the design, not an oversight**: the webs this factory builds are required to live under
`spider-factory/webs/` — `webs/webs.yml` and `scripts/clone-webs.sh` are what put them there — and a tower is a
clone of one of those webs, so a tower outside the factory is not a state the system supports and the refusal is
the system saying so. **What that costs, kept on the record:** the refusal is a `die` to stderr, which reaches
the hook's debug output and never `$SF_LOG_ROOT/<run>/`, so a worker whose tower does sit outside the factory
leaves no line anywhere in the run's logs. A person's own session
never reaches the hook at all, wherever it stands: the event is bound in the seven worker agents' own
frontmatter, and a plain session is run as none of the seven. Only a session that reaches the hook as one of those
seven does, and it always stands in that hook's own worktree.

**What is not a command.** Anything starting with `-` is not a command at all — it goes to the bare session as
arguments. Anything else unknown is refused **by name**, with the seven listed and `Try 'sf --help'` under it,
exit 1 — and `sf hook` refuses anything but its one arm the same way, naming it and saying `there is one:
'sf hook stop'`. There is no webs command: the
cloner beside this one is an npm script, not a `sf` one.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/bin/sf "Source — CLI/bin/sf"
    }

    installer = cliTool "CLI/install-cli.sh — put sf on your path" {
      summary "One bash script you run yourself: it writes the `sf` launcher at `~/.local/bin/sf` and, the first time only, the parameters file."
      description """
**What it is:** `CLI/install-cli.sh` — one bash file, no node, no package manager, and no agent in the
loop of it: you run `bash CLI/install-cli.sh` and it locates the repo from its own path, so the directory
you happen to stand in does not matter. This is the whole route to `sf` on your path. It writes two things.

**Write 1 — the launcher: one real file at `~/.local/bin/sf`, never a symlink.** A real file is
deliberate — it outlives the repo being moved or deleted, and it names its own problem. Its header bakes in
the repo path (`# Repo: <path>`) and the line `written by spider-factory's CLI/install-cli.sh`, which is
what the uninstaller greps for. On a later run, if `<repo>/CLI/bin/sf` is gone, the launcher itself prints
the path it is missing, says nothing else on your system changed, and prints both ways out: the `rm -f` that
clears the launcher, or clone the repo again and run its `CLI/install-cli.sh` again. Otherwise it execs
`bash <repo>/CLI/bin/sf` with your arguments. Re-running the installer overwrites the launcher — that is how
you repoint it after moving the repo.

**Write 2 — `CLI/spider-factory.example.env` copied to `CLI/spider-factory.env`, `chmod 600`, and only when
that file is missing.** Once it exists the installer never touches it again: those are your edits by then,
and deleting the file is how you get the defaults back. It does **read** it, to compare: every key the shipped
example carries that the kept file lacks is counted and named on stderr, with the two ways out — add them by
hand, or delete the file and re-run. A file kept for a year and an example that gained a key since is otherwise
indistinguishable from a setting nobody wrote. The announce line says which of the two happened —
`wrote the example` or `kept your edits`. What the settings are is the parameters node's, not this one's.

**Then one warning and two lines, and both installers speak through `console.sh`.** If `~/.local/bin` is not
on your `PATH` it prints the exact `export PATH=` line your shell profile is missing, rather than installing
something you cannot reach. Each installer calls `nameTheSpeaker sf`, so the warning comes out `sf: …` and
the two announce lines — the launcher it wrote and the env file's state — come out unprefixed.

**Taking it back off: `CLI/uninstall-cli.sh` removes that launcher and nothing else.** No launcher at all →
it says so and exits 0. A launcher carrying no marker of this repo's → it refuses, leaves it alone, and
hands you the `rm -f` to run yourself if it really is yours; that grep is what keeps it off somebody else's
`sf`. The parameters file survives either way. Deleting the ticket database is not an uninstall and no
script here does it.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/install-cli.sh "Source — CLI/install-cli.sh"
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/uninstall-cli.sh "Source — CLI/uninstall-cli.sh"
    }

    websCloner = cliTool "npm run webs:clone — the webs the factory builds" {
      summary "Clone every repo url in `webs/webs.yml` into `webs/` beside it. Never pulls, never resets, never deletes; `sf` has no webs command."
      description """
**What it is:** `scripts/clone-webs.sh`, run by `npm run webs:clone` and by nothing else — `sf` has no webs
command, and the installer never touches it. Every web the workers build is a clone sitting in `webs/`.

| Fact | Where it comes from |
|---|---|
| the list | `webs/webs.yml` — one repo url a line under `repos:`, each line starting with a dash; a `#` comments a line out, and quotes around a url are stripped |
| its first copy | copied from the tracked `webs/webs.example.yml` **only when it is missing** — nothing else ever writes that file, not the installer and not `sf`; delete it and the next run writes the shipped one back |
| where a clone lands | `webs/<last segment of the url, .git stripped>` |
| what is in the repo | `webs/.gitkeep` and `webs.example.yml` alone — `webs/*` is gitignored, so a clone belongs to the machine and never to the repo |

**One line per url, then it moves on.** `cloned name=… url=…`; `skipped name=… reason=already-cloned` when a
`.git` is already there, or `reason=not-a-clone` when something else is; `failed name=… reason=git-clone`.
Two more refuse before anything is attempted: `reason=not-a-git-url` when the url does not start `https://`,
`http://`, `ssh://`, `git://` or read like `user@host:path`, and `reason=no-directory-name` when the url names
no folder. An existing directory is left exactly as it is — **never pulls, never resets, never deletes.**

One summary line closes the run, `webs: N cloned, M skipped, K failed into <webs/>`; a list with nothing in it
says so out loud instead of cloning nothing quietly. The run exits non-zero only when something failed.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/scripts/clone-webs.sh "Source — scripts/clone-webs.sh"
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/webs/webs.example.yml "Source — webs/webs.example.yml"
    }

    preflight = noteDoc "The checks — everything that must hold before the first tick" {
      summary "The seven checks that must hold before the first tick; six of them refuse the run outright."
      description """
In the order the code runs them — the bootstrap at the bottom of `CLI/src/beads-loop/index.sh`, which calls
them one after another; checks 2–5 live in `preflight.sh` beside it, check 6 is `requireBeadsProject`, which lives
in `CLI/utils/console.sh` beside `die` and is the one beads probe every command runs, checks 1 and 7 in `config.sh`.
Six of the seven
end the run through `die` — `beads-loop: <what is wrong>` on stderr, exit 1 — and no tick ever happens; a run that
cannot report on itself is not a run. `die` is not theirs: it lives in `CLI/utils/console.sh`, the one file in the CLI
that writes to a terminal — every engine, `bin/sf`, `CLI/utils/user-env.sh` and both installers source it and each
script calls `nameTheSpeaker <name>`
once, so every line each one says comes out `<name>: <line>`; the loader sources it for exactly that reason, so it
can name a line of the parameters file it refuses to read. Check 2 refuses through `reportToolNotOnPath` and
check 6 through `reportNoBeadsDatabase`; that second sentence is the one `sf`, `plan-agent.sh` and
`overseer-agent.sh` refuse with too, and `reportMissingFromInstall` (a file the install should have put down) and
`reportAgentCommandNotOnPath` (the first word of `SF_AGENT_COMMAND`) are the other two reused refusals.
Checks 3–5 pass their own sentence to `die` directly.

| # | Check | Refuses when |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `configureAllowedLabels` — `SF_ALLOWED_LABELS` against the `AGENT` map | **not fatal** — a typo'd list matches no ticket, and the tower sits still while waiting lines keep arriving |
| 2 | `requireToolsOnPath` — `bd jq git` plus the first word of `SF_AGENT_COMMAND` | the first one missing is named on stderr |
| 3 | `requireNumericSettingsFromEnvironment` | `SF_MAX_PARALLEL` / `SF_POLL_S` / `SF_MAX_TICKETS` set to anything but digits — never defaulted silently, because they govern unattended workers |
| 4 | `requireGateFlagsAreSwitches` — `SF_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES` and `SF_AGENT_EPIC_GATES`, each read by `configureFromEnvironment` | either one is anything but `0` or `1` — the line names the flag and says what each value means: `0` a human closes that gate, `1` the lead agent does. A half-open gate tier is not a state the loop will guess its way through |
| 5 | `requireUsablePollInterval` — `SF_POLL_S` must divide 60 | the permitted list is printed: `1 2 3 4 5 6 10 12 15 20 30 60`; the waiting lines are rate-limited at one per minute, so a poll that does not divide the minute makes that cadence a lie |
| 6 | `requireBeadsProject` — `requireBeadsToolsOnPath` first, then `bd list --limit 1 --json` must answer an **array** | a missing `bd` or `jq` is named as a missing binary, never as a missing database; then `bd` walks **up** from the cwd and ignores `BEADS_DIR`, so a database in the environment does not save a wrong directory |
| 7 | `createRunLogDirectory` — `mkdir -p` the run's dir, then seed `claims errors waiting results closes` | fatal on purpose: a run that cannot write its logs cannot raise an alarm |

`sf` itself runs check 6 and two before it — `requireInsideFactory`, that the directory it was typed in is
the factory root or anything under it, and `requireEngine`, that `../src/beads-loop/index.sh` is actually
there — so a broken install names itself instead of failing deep in a tick.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/src/beads-loop/index.sh#L122-L135 "Source — beads-loop/index.sh lines 122–135, the bootstrap that runs them in order"
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/src/beads-loop/preflight.sh#L5-L43 "Source — beads-loop/preflight.sh lines 5–43, checks 2–6"
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/utils/console.sh "Source — CLI/utils/console.sh, die, the four reused refusals and the one beads probe"
    }

    knobs = knobPanel "The run's parameters (env, read once at startup)" {
      summary "The run's parameters, read once at startup out of `CLI/spider-factory.env`; what you export yourself still wins."
      description """
Both engines source `CLI/utils/user-env.sh` and call `loadSpiderFactoryEnv` as their first act. It takes **any**
`KEY=value` line out of `CLI/spider-factory.env` (`CLI/spider-factory.example.env` copied there on install) — not only
`SF_*=` ones, so a knob the engines read by name works whatever it is called — expands `$HOME` in a
value, and **exports only what is unset** — so a value you exported yourself still beats the file.
A line that is neither blank, nor a comment, nor `KEY=value` is **named on stderr with its file and line number and
skipped**, never dropped in silence; the loader sources `console.sh` so it has `tell` to do that with.
`SF_ENV_FILE` points it elsewhere, which is the whole test hook; no file at all is a normal state, and
the engines' own `:-` defaults are the floor under it. `CLI/spider-factory.example.env` is documentation that happens
to be installed, so it and the engines are kept in step by hand.

**The one thing the loader writes whatever the file says is `SF_AGENT_LAUNCH_COMMAND`.** It is the last line of
`loadSpiderFactoryEnv`, runs whether or not the env file exists, and overwrites anything already there: your
`SF_AGENT_COMMAND` plus the flags this repo hardcodes into every agent spawn — today `--add-dir <factory root>`,
the root derived from `user-env.sh`'s own `BASH_SOURCE`. Composed once there so no spawn site carries a flag of
its own. It is **not a setting**: nothing reads it out of `CLI/spider-factory.env`, and hand-editing it buys
nothing, because the next load replaces it. `SF_AGENT_COMMAND` stays the knob you edit. What the extra directory
is for is on the agent-command node.

| Knob | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `SF_AGENT_COMMAND` | `claude` | the command that runs an agent — **the knob you edit**, and the one `CLI/spider-factory.example.env` documents; split on spaces, so it may carry its own arguments. Read directly by the worker count and by the startup `PATH` check; every actual spawn goes through the launch command below |
| `SF_AGENT_LAUNCH_COMMAND` | *(not a setting)* | `$SF_AGENT_COMMAND --add-dir <factory root>`, exported by `loadSpiderFactoryEnv` on every load and overwritten each time — what the worker spawn, the three planners and the overseer actually exec. Listed here because it is `SF_*` and because editing it does nothing |
| `SF_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES` | `0` | does the **lead agent** close a feature gate for you. `0` = a human closes it; `1` = the loop dispatches the lead agent at a ready feature gate. Anything else and the run refuses to start |
| `SF_AGENT_EPIC_GATES` | `0` | the same switch for the epic gate alone — one flag per tier, because a feature gate and the epic gate are different amounts of trust |
| `SF_MAX_PARALLEL` | `4` | workers running at once; the count is recounted from `$SF_AGENT_COMMAND agents --json` every tick, and a session counts only while it reports itself `busy` — a finished worker holds no slot |
| `SF_POLL_S` | `3` | seconds between ticks; **must divide 60** or the run refuses to start, because the idle rate limit is `60 / SF_POLL_S` ticks |
| `SF_MAX_TICKETS` | `0` | stop once N tickets have closed this run — counted off the run's `closes` file, so the loop keeps ticking while it waits; 0 = endless |
| `SF_LOG_ROOT` | `~/.spider-factory/logs` | parent of the one timestamped dir this run writes |
| `SF_ALLOWED_LABELS` | unset or `*` = the whole AGENT set | a comma list narrows which labels may dispatch |
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/spider-factory.example.env "Source — CLI/spider-factory.example.env"
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/utils/user-env.sh "Source — CLI/utils/user-env.sh"
    }

    dispatcher = dispatcher "beads-loop/ — the dispatcher" {
      summary "The engine: a tick dispatches any ready gate its tier flag opens, then claims one ready ticket, derives its branch off the id, spawns its worker."
      description """
**What it is:** the engine `sf loop` runs. A `while true` loop: test the ticket cap, `tick`, `sleep $SF_POLL_S`,
again. It ends by itself only when `SF_MAX_TICKETS` tickets have closed this run — the test is against the run's
`closes` file, not against the tick count, so a run waiting on workers still ticking is never cut off.
It is the only thing in the system that claims, dispatches, publishes the epic branch, or owns folders.

**Where the engine lives:** `CLI/src/beads-loop/` — ten bash files, one concern each, no node, no build.
`index.sh` is the file `sf loop` executes and the only one with a bootstrap: the knobs, the `AGENT` map, the
order the other nine are sourced in, `claimAndDispatchOneTicket`, `tick()`, `runLoop()`, the interrupt trap.
Beside it: `config.sh` the knobs, the waiting cadence, the tower paths, the log folder · `preflight.sh` the
checks that end the run before the first tick, and the gate tier flags · `logs.sh` the human text of every
message, so no call site carries a sentence · `tower-id.sh` the id grammar and everything derived off it · `git.sh` the worktree, the epic branch
and its PR · `beads.sh` every beads read that is not a claim · `claim.sh` the frontier, the guards a claim must
pass, the waiting line · `worker.sh` the prompt, the spawn, the dispatch · `gates.sh` the gate side of the tick.
Every function named below lives in one of these. **What the run says to the terminal is nobody's business
here:** that is `CLI/utils/console.sh`, sourced from `../utils/` beside `logging.sh` (`forge.sh` stays beside
`index.sh` in `CLI/src/`), which `index.sh` names `beads-loop` — the startup announcement and the closing
summary are its `announceRunLogDirectory` and `reportStopped`, and every other word a run says is a log line
first, which `writeLog` also echoes while the loop runs.

One tick, in the order the code runs it (`tick()`, `index.sh`):

| # | Step | Reads | On failure |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **dispatch any ready gate its tier flag opens** — `dispatchReadyGates`, before a single ticket is claimed. Returns at once when neither `SF_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES` nor `SF_AGENT_EPIC_GATES` is `1` | `bd list --include-gates --ready --type gate --limit 0 --json` → `.id`; the tier is `gateTierOf`, never a key | see the gate rows below |
| 2 | capacity check — once per tick, before the claim, and the tick ends there when it fails | `$SF_AGENT_COMMAND agents --json`, background sessions reporting `status: busy` whose `cwd` starts with `<tower>/.claude/worktrees` or equals the tower | waiting `max_parallel` / `max_parallel_stalled`, and the tick ends |
| 3 | **claim** — one claim a tick, and once it has been read the tick is over | `bd ready --claim --json` — atomic: assignee + `in_progress` + a 5-minute lease | `bd-failure`; an empty answer is a waiting line, not an error |
| 4 | match labels against the `AGENT` map — **exactly one** may match | `.[0].labels` | `label-ambiguous`, left claimed |
| 5 | derive the branch and the PR target off the **id** | `resolveTicketBranches <id>` — `splitTowerId` reads `<repo>-<kind>-<uuid>-<hierarchy>` off the right; the uuid names the tower, the kind word swapped to `feature` with the hierarchy cut at the last dot is the feature gate whose `branch` the work lands on, the kind word swapped to `epic` at hierarchy `0` the epic gate whose `branch` is `prTarget`; `prTarget` is `main` when the owner is the epic gate itself. Nothing is walked and no key on the ticket is read | `no-epic-gate ticket=…` (id does not read the grammar · `step=not-below-a-gate` · `gate=<the epic it names>`), `gate-unworkable ticket=…` (`owner-carries-no-branch` · `epic-carries-no-branch`), left claimed |
| 6 | publish the epic branch + PR if the gate has none | `ensureEpicGatePr <gate-id>` — the branch is born with a commit of its own, so it is never `main`'s own commit | `epic-pr-failed step=…`, `bd unclaim` |
| 7 | make the branch's folder | `ensureWorktree branch prTarget` | `worktree-failed`, left claimed |
| 8 | spawn | `$SF_AGENT_LAUNCH_COMMAND --bg --agent <agent> --permission-mode bypassPermissions` — your `SF_AGENT_COMMAND` plus the flags every spawn carries (`--add-dir <factory root>`), the bare agent name, a project-level agent in `.claude/agents/` | `worker-not-dispatched`, `bd unclaim` |
| 9 | one log line, move on | `writeLog claims \"reason=dispatched\"` | — |

**One pair of hands per branch is the tower's job, not the loop's.** Every ticket below a feature gate sits on
that gate's one branch in a single linked line of `blocks` edges, so only one of them is ever ready and only one
is ever `in_progress` on that branch; the same holds at the epic. The loop runs **no busy-branch check**: it
reads the frontier once a tick, never puts a claim back to try another, and never counts what is already at
work. A tower that fans into two ready tickets on one branch is a mis-built tower, and the loop works both.

**The gate half of the tick** (`dispatchOneGate`, step 1) is the only place a worker is spawned without a
label. A gate never appears in `bd ready`, so it is claimed **by status**: `bd update <gate> --status
in_progress --if-status open`, and the `--if-status` is both the race guard and what takes the gate off the
ready list until its lead agent dies.

| Step | Reads | On failure |
|---|---|---|
| the tier is worked out, then switched on | `gateTierOf` — the kind word of the gate's own id: `epic` or `feature`, nothing read from the database; matched against the two flags | a tier whose flag is `0` is skipped in silence; an id carrying neither word logs `gate-unworkable gate=…` |
| derive the gate's branch and target | `resolveTicketBranches <gate>` — the gate's own `branch`, and the epic gate's `branch` (or `main`) as the target | waiting `gate_unworkable gate=… step=…` |
| **claim it** | `bd update <gate> --status in_progress --if-status open` | `gate-claim-lost`, no lead agent spawned |
| make the branch's folder | `ensureWorktree gateBranch gatePrTarget` | `worktree-failed step=gate`, and the gate is put back `open` |
| spawn the **lead agent** | `--agent lead`, the bare name; label on the line is `gate:<tier>` | `worker-not-dispatched gate=…`, the gate put back `open` |
| one log line | `writeLog claims \"reason=dispatched kind=gate gate=… tier=…\"` | — |

**What it never does:** it never polls a worker, never reads worker output, never merges, never ends
itself, never closes a ticket, and never derives an agent name from a label. It also runs **no
`bd reclaim`**: a lease that lapses leaves its ticket parked `in_progress`, off the frontier — sweeping
claims back is the supervisor's job, not the loop's.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/src/beads-loop/index.sh "Source — CLI/src/beads-loop/index.sh, the tick and the run"
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/tree/main/CLI/src/beads-loop "Source — the whole CLI/src/beads-loop/ directory, the ten files"
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/utils/console.sh "Source — CLI/utils/console.sh, the run's terminal lines"
    }

    agentMap = noteDoc "The AGENT map — the only label→worker binding" {
      summary "The seven labels that dispatch, each bound to one worker; nothing else picks a worker."
      description """
```bash
declare -A AGENT=(
    [backend-logic]=engineer
    [frontend-ui]=frontend
    [unit-tests]=tester-unit
    [feature-test]=tester-feature
    [epic-e2e-test]=tester-e2e
    [feature-review]=review
    [epic-review]=review
)

leadAgentName="lead"
```

**Three names, never interchangeable** — collapsing any two is how a tower silently dispatches nothing:

| Name | What it is | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| **category** | a column of plan-task's own node-type table. Nothing ever writes one to beads — no `--category` is passed anywhere in this repo | `backend-logic`, `frontend-ux`, `unit-tests` — the three plan-task node types, and nothing above a task carries one |
| **label** | written with `bd create -l`; a ticket may carry any number, but **exactly one** must be a key of this map | the seven keys above, bar none |
| **agent** | a real file in `.claude/agents/` | `engineer`, `frontend`, `tester-unit`, `tester-feature`, `tester-e2e`, `review`, `lead` |

The keys **are** the closed label set: `isLabel` requires membership here *and* in the allowed set.
Zero or two matches on a claim is the tower's bug, not the loop's — it never guesses a specialist.
`review` answers two labels; the three test labels are three **tiers**, and the label — never `--agent` —
is what carries the tier, so a mis-labelled ticket gets the wrong depth of coverage and no worker can
tell you it did. Note `frontend-ui` is the **label** while `frontend-ux` is only its category: a ticket
labelled with its category matches nothing. There is no merge label at either tier — nothing assembles a
feature or an epic, each branch is merged by the **lead agent** standing on the gate above it, and the
lead agent is reached by the gate dispatch, not by a key of this map.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/src/beads-loop/index.sh#L21-L31 "Source — beads-loop/index.sh lines 21–31, the AGENT map and the lead agent's name"
    }

    hookFields = knobPanel "SF_* — the nine log fields the Stop hook fills itself" {
      summary "The SF_ variables the Stop hook exports inside its own process, filled off this session's dispatched claims line. The spawn sets none."
      description """
**The spawn writes no environment at all.** `spawnWorker` (`CLI/src/beads-loop/worker.sh`) runs the agent command
with nothing in front of it — no `SF_TICKET=`, no `SF_BRANCH=`, none of them. `claude --bg` serves every session
from one shared daemon that **freezes the environment at the run's first spawn**, so anything the loop set there
would reach a later worker, and its Stop hook, as the *first* worker's values. The facts travel two other ways: to
the **worker** as literals in its prompt — ticket id, branch, and the tower's path, plus `prTarget` in the lead
agent's rule 4 — and to
the **CLI** as the dispatch record the loop writes around the spawn: the worker-context file
`<log root>/worker-context/<worktree folder name>`, written **before** the agent starts, and the
`reason=dispatched` line in the run's `claims` file, written once its session id is known.

**The fields below are the hook's own, in the hook's own process.** `adoptDispatchContext` takes the session id out
of the hook's stdin JSON, matches it to this session's claims line, and exports each field off that line — the
context file only as the fallback for what a thin line omits. Two shells have two environments and nothing carries
between them: there is no cross-process persistence and no scratch file. The working directory is the one thing it
does inherit true — a claimed spare is chdir'd to the spawn's cwd, so the hook stands in the worktree — but a
worktree belongs to a branch and not to a ticket, so it identifies nothing. What the hook cannot rebuild, it
refuses: no ticket or no run is `reason=claim-malformed`, no branch `reason=no-branch`, no target
`reason=no-pr-target`, and the ticket stays claimed.

| Field | Value | Where the hook gets it | Who reads it |
|---|---|---|---|
| `SF_TICKET` | `the back-end ticket` | claims line `ticket=` | what the close, the commit and every alarm are about. It is **not** the test that picks a dispatched session out of all the others: that is the claims line itself |
| `SF_BRANCH` | `feature/add-record` | claims line `branch=`, else context file `branch=` | commit here, push here, refuse if `git branch --show-current` disagrees |
| `SF_PR_TARGET` | `epic/record-box`, or `main` at the epic tier | claims line `target=`, else context file `pr_target=` | the target of this branch's PR — derived off the ticket's id by the loop |
| `SF_AGENT` | `engineer`, or `lead` on a gate dispatch | claims line `agent=`, else context file `agent=` | so an alarm names who was doing the work |
| `SF_LABEL` | `backend-logic`, or `gate:<tier>` on a gate dispatch | claims line `label=`, else context file `label=` | so an alarm names what chose that worker |
| `SF_PROJECT_DIR` | the tower root, where `.beads` lives | claims line `repo=`, else context file `tower=` | every `bd -C` the hook runs — **the worktree is not the tower** |
| `SF_LOG_DIR` | this run's log dir | the claims line's `run=`, under the log root | alarms land in `$SF_LOG_DIR/errors` |
| `SF_SESSION` | the session id | the hook's own stdin JSON, `.session_id` | the key that found the claims line, and the `--session` the close is filed under |
| `SF_WORKTREE` | the branch's folder | claims line `worktree=` | the folder every `git -C` the hook runs stands in |

`SF_LOG_QUIET=1` is exported by the Stop hook before it writes its first line: a hook must never print
into a session. The engine leaves it unset, so its own lines also go to the terminal. Nothing else is handed to
the worker, and the worker writes nothing to signal it is finished: what ends its turn is the `Stop` event, and the
`claims` line is what says the turn was the loop's to begin with.

**The ninth dispatch fact, `kind`, is not one of the nine log fields.** `spawnWorker` takes it as its ninth
argument, `${9:-task}`, and writes it into the context file beside the other six — `ticket branch pr_target agent
label tower kind`; a **gate** dispatch additionally writes `kind=gate` onto its claims line as detail. The Stop hook
reads it back as `SF_WORK_KIND="${claimKind:-${kind:-task}}"`, off the claims line first and the context file only
as a fallback. It is the only thing that tells the hook it is finishing a **gate** rather than a task.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/src/beads-loop/worker.sh#L54-L68 "Source — beads-loop/worker.sh lines 54–68, spawnWorker: the context file, then the spawn with no environment in front of it"
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/src/agent-hooks/worker-stop.sh#L82-L117 "Source — CLI/src/agent-hooks/worker-stop.sh lines 82–117, adoptDispatchContext: the claims line becomes the hook's own nine fields"
    }

    claim = cliTool "bd ready --claim — the hand that takes one ticket" {
      summary "bd ready --claim — takes one unblocked ticket atomically and starts its five-minute lease."
      description """
**What it is:** the one atomic read in the system, and the only reason the loop can run two places at once
without stealing its own work. `bd ready --claim --json` picks the single first unblocked ticket and, in the
same statement, stamps an assignee, moves it to `in_progress`, and starts a **five-minute lease**.

| Property | Consequence |
|---|---|
| atomic | a bare `bd ready` is a peek, not a take — two loops reading with it would both work the same ticket |
| one ticket per call | and the tick makes exactly **one** of them: read the claim, dispatch or refuse, and the tick is over — there is no second claim waiting behind it |
| an **empty** answer is not an error | that is a waiting line: `frontier_empty` if nothing is waiting elsewhere, `tower_stalled waiting=N` if work exists behind a blocker |
| the lease is the only watchdog | a lapsed lease parks the ticket `in_progress` and off the frontier — nothing in this repo runs `bd reclaim`, so sweeping claims back is a human's act |
| `bd unclaim` puts a claim back | `releaseClaim` is one line, called from **two** places: the epic branch and its PR could not be published · the spawn died. Everything else that refuses — a mislabelled ticket, an id that reaches no gate, a worktree that could not be made — leaves the claim where it is, on purpose |

A claim is a JSON object, and **two** fields decide everything downstream: `.id` (which two gates it sits
under, and therefore which branch the work lands on and which its pull request aims at — `resolveTicketBranches`
reads both off the id, and a second query fetches the gates' own `branch` keys) and `.labels` (which worker, if
exactly one matches the map). Nothing on the claim's `.metadata` is read at all. Anything else on the ticket —
title, description, notes — the loop never reads.

**A gate never appears here.** `bd ready` does not list one, so a gate is never taken by this command; the
loop moves it to `in_progress` itself with `bd update <gate> --status in_progress --if-status open`, which is
step 1 of the tick and the only claim in the system made by status.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/src/beads-loop/claim.sh "Source — CLI/src/beads-loop/claim.sh, the frontier and the guards"
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/src/beads-loop/gates.sh#L13-L47 "Source — CLI/src/beads-loop/gates.sh lines 13–47, dispatchOneGate: the claim made by status"
    }

  }  // machine

  logRun = logStore "~/.spider-factory/logs/<date>_<time> — one dir per run" {
    summary "One timestamped directory per run, holding every file that run writes."
    description """
**What it is:** the central home for everything a run leaves behind. The engine seeds **five** files up
front (`claims errors waiting results closes`), so a missing file and an empty file look the
same and neither hides a silent failure — `closes` is written by the Stop hook, possibly long after the run
ended. Two more appear only when something writes them (`bd-stderr`, from the claim, and `claude-stderr`, from
the capacity count), and one per worker (`spawn-<ticket>`).

**No sixth file is seeded, and none of these five ever holds a line about a worker that died.** A sixth used to
be, `failures`, seeded alongside the rest and written by nothing at all, so an empty one read as "no failures"
when it meant "no such feature" — it is gone from the seed list and from the code. What it stood for has not
gone, and the absence is a louder alarm than the file ever was: `Stop` is the only hook event a worker agent
binds and there is no `StopFailure` to bind, so a session killed by an API error fires no hook and writes no
line into any file in this directory. The tell is the **absence across** the five — a `claims` line, no `closes`
line, and a ticket left `in_progress` that no `bd ready` will ever name. The
overseer's check for a worker that died quietly is the only thing that finds one, and all it can do is name the
ticket and hand a human `bd update <ticket> --status failed-agent` to run.

**Beside the run dirs, not inside one: `<log root>/worker-context/<worktree folder name>`.** This is the other half
of the dispatch record and the only state in the system that is neither the database nor a log line. The loop writes
it before the agent starts, one file per **branch** (the folder name is the branch with `/` → `+`), holding
`ticket= branch= pr_target= agent= label= tower= kind=`; a second dispatch onto the same branch replaces it, and the
Stop hook deletes it the moment the ticket closes. It is not seeded, not per run, and never read by a tick — only by
the hook, as the fallback for what a thin claims line omits, and by `sf commit`, which reads its `agent=` key to
name the commit subject. Nothing under it is safe to delete mid-run: a missing file plus a thin claims line is
`reason=no-context`, and the ticket stays claimed.

**One line format, and one owner of it** (`CLI/utils/logging.sh`), or the files cannot be read against each
other. **Two callers source that one file, and nothing else does.** The engine sources it and keeps its nine
fields in **process memory** for the whole run, setting them as the tick goes. The Stop hook sources the same
file and fills the same nine fields from its own environment on every line, through
`takeLogFieldsFromEnvironment`.

For a caller with no process memory of its own those nine come from **one variable per key**: `SF_LOG_RUN`
(falling back to the name of the log directory), `SF_PROJECT_DIR`, `SF_TICKET`, `SF_LABEL`, `SF_AGENT`,
`SF_BRANCH`, `SF_PR_TARGET`, `SF_SESSION`, `SF_WORKTREE`. Export them once in your own process and every line
after carries them; one you never set comes out `-`. Nothing carries from one process to the next — two shells are
two environments — so the hook fills them off the dispatched claims line it just matched to this session, with the
dispatch context file as the fallback for what a thin line omits (the nine, and where each one comes from, are on
the hook-fields node). It cannot inherit them from the worker: the spawn hands the session **no environment at
all**, because `claude --bg` runs every session through a pooled daemon that froze the environment at the run's
first spawn. The engine exports none of them either — it keeps its own nine in process memory with `setLogField`.
The directory the lines land in is `$logDir`: the engine's own, and the hook rebuilds it from `SF_LOG_DIR`, then
`SF_LOG_ROOT`, then the default.

```
<TAG padded to 8> <ISO time>\trun=… repo=… ticket=… label=… agent=… branch=… target=… session=… worktree=… <detail>
```

The nine keys are on **every line of every file**, `\"-\"` for whatever is not known at that moment — an
absent field would be indistinguishable from a field nobody set, and an error line is worth nothing if
it cannot say what it happened to. That shared key set is what makes the files **JOIN**: an `errors` line
names the ticket, label, agent, branch, target, repo and session, and the same keys lead from it into
`claims` or `closes` by `grep` alone. Anything outside the nine — a `step=`, a `gate=`, an
`exit=`, a `msg=` — is passed as **detail**; the key set is closed, or the joining stops working.

```
CLAIMS   2026-11-04T09:12:03\trun=2026-11-04_09-12-01 repo=/home/dev/spider-factory/webs/record-box ticket=back-end label=backend-logic agent=engineer branch=feature/add-record target=epic/record-box session=287d3c4c worktree=/home/dev/spider-factory/webs/record-box/.claude/worktrees/feature+add-record reason=dispatched
```
"""
    link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/utils/logging.sh "Source — CLI/utils/logging.sh, the one definition of a line"

    fClaims = logFile "claims" {
      summary "Every ticket taken, and the epic branch the first time it is published."
      description """
Every ticket the loop took. `reason=dispatched` on a spawn, and `gate=… reason=epic-published gateBranch=…
pr=…` when the loop publishes the epic branch — that one line names no ticket of its own, because the identity
the tick already carries is on it. A claim the loop puts back (`bd unclaim`) writes **nothing here**: the
release is silent, so a ticket that was claimed and handed back leaves no trace in this file.

A **gate** dispatch is the dispatched line with three keys added: `reason=dispatched kind=gate gate=<id>
tier=<epic|feature>`, and its fixed identity already reads `ticket=<gate id> agent=lead label=gate:<tier>
target=<the gate's `prTarget`>`. Those three keys are the only place a reader can tell a gate dispatch from a task
dispatch — the nine identity keys look identical either way, and the Stop hook reads `kind=` off this very
line to decide which path to take.
"""
    }
    fErrors = logFile "errors" {
      summary "Every failure, whoever saw it, and never rate-limited."
      description """
The alarm: every failure, whoever saw it. **Never rate-limited** — a waiting line is capped at one a minute
per reason, an error never is.
"""
    }
    fWaiting = logFile "waiting" {
      summary "The quiet ticks, one line a minute per reason."
      description """
The quiet ticks, **one a minute per reason** (`60 / SF_POLL_S` ticks), each carrying
`working=<busy sessions>` and `next=<the id a bare bd ready would name>`.
"""
    }
    fCloses = logFile "closes" {
      summary "One line per ticket that landed, with its pull request url."
      description """
The Stop hook's proof of work: one line per ticket that landed, `url=<the PR>`. A **gate** close carries one
extra key, `url=<the PR> gate=true` — the same url, because the lead agent merged that branch's own pull
request rather than opening a new one, and the hook found it whatever state it was in.
"""
    }
    fResults = logFile "results" {
      summary "The run's tally of ticks, claims and tickets closed, at the ticket cap or on Ctrl-C."
      description """
The run's tally — `ticks=N claimed=M done=K` — at `SF_MAX_TICKETS`, or on Ctrl-C (`onInterrupt`). The cap line
carries a `note=` saying the run was configured to end there; the Ctrl-C line does not, so the two are
told apart without guessing. `done=K` is the count off this run's `closes` file.
"""
    }
    fBdStderr = logFile "bd-stderr" {
      summary "Stderr of the last claim, quoted on a claim failure."
      description """
Stderr of the last `bd ready --claim`, captured on the claim line itself; its last line is quoted as
`msg=` on a `bd-failure` alarm.
"""
    }
    fClaudeStderr = logFile "claude-stderr" {
      summary "Stderr of the last capacity count."
      description """
Stderr of the last `$SF_AGENT_COMMAND agents --json` capacity count.
"""
    }
    fSpawn = logFile "spawn-<ticket>" {
      summary "That one worker's raw spawn output, which holds its session id."
      description """
That one worker's raw spawn output, which holds its session id (`backgrounded · 287d3c4c`), read back by
`getSpawnedAgent`. It used to be one shared `last-spawn` that every spawn overwrote, so the id survived
only for the run's final worker — the one thing a human needs to attach to, read, or stop.
"""
    }
  }

  stopHook = hook "worker-stop.sh — the Stop hook, fail-closed" {
    summary "The closer, registered by each worker agent's own frontmatter: commit, push, the branch's PR, close — seventeen refusals first."
    description """
**What it is:** the closer, and **it runs**. It sits at `CLI/src/agent-hooks/worker-stop.sh`, an engine beside every
other one that `bin/sf` locates and execs, and implements the whole
finish below. The registration is in the agent files themselves: each of the **seven worker agents** —
`engineer`, `frontend`, `tester-unit`, `tester-feature`, `tester-e2e`, `review`, `lead` — carries a `hooks:` block
in its YAML frontmatter binding `Stop` → `sf hook stop` (timeout 300), and the harness reaches this script through
that command, the seventh arm of `sf` — and its only arm: `sf hook` refuses a second word by name. There is no `hooks.json` — that file is gone — and no
`.claude/settings.json` registers anything: an agent's own frontmatter is the only wiring. `overseer.md` and the
three `plan-*.md` agents deliberately carry no such block, so a session run as one of those four never fires it.
The event that arrives is **`Stop`, not `SubagentStop`**: `claude --bg --agent <name>` is a top-level session with
an agent attached, not a subagent, which is why the binding sits under `Stop:`.

What tells a dispatched worker apart from everything else is the `claims` line that names this session id: a
session with no dispatched claims line leaves at once, silently, and writes nothing anywhere. Never the cwd — the
hook input's `.cwd` is the worktree and is real, a claimed spare being chdir'd to the spawn's cwd, but a worktree
belongs to a branch and not to a ticket, so it identifies no worker.

```
the worker's session ends (`Stop`) → `sf commit` onto its feature's branch → push → the branch's PR →
write the PR url onto the ticket's pr metadata → close it
```

It refuses **seventeen** ways, each one an `errors` line then `exit 0` with the ticket untouched, in the order the
code checks them —
they are walked one by one in the second walkthrough. Nothing anywhere says a worker's brief is finished: there is no
marker, no declaration, no signal of the worker's own that the hook could check. The `Stop` event is the only thing
that says a turn ended, and the dispatched `claims` line is the only thing that says the turn was the loop's to
begin with — once this session is matched to that line, landing the work is what the hook does. What that costs is
the honesty of the stop: a worker that quits early still gets its commit, its push, its PR and its closed ticket,
carrying whatever it managed, so the diff, the worker's final message and whatever it wrote on its own ticket before
it stopped are the only things a person has to judge the tier by. The final message dies with the session; the
comment on the ticket is the part of that report that outlives it — the duty itself is the workers layer's, not this
hook's, and this hook neither writes one nor checks that one exists. That is why the contract's last bullet is about
when it is honest to stop.

**It writes its own lines.** The script sources `CLI/utils/logging.sh` — the one definition of a line — directly,
the way the engine does, and nothing else sources it. Its own `logLine <tag> [detail…]` sets the directory from
`SF_LOG_DIR`, then `SF_LOG_ROOT`, then the default, makes sure it exists, fills the nine fields from its own
environment with `takeLogFieldsFromEnvironment`, and calls `writeLog`. It exports `SF_LOG_QUIET=1` before its
first line, so a hook never prints at a terminal it does not own. There is no `sf log` command to call: that
command is gone, and a hook that shelled out to it would be a hook that logs nothing.

**The commit is the worker's own command, not a private one:** `stageTheWork` first asks whether anything is
left to land — usually the worker committed it all as it went, and then nothing is — and `commitTheTask` runs
`sf commit` in the worktree with the ticket's title and a `Ticket: <id>` trailer as the message, so a task's last
commit is the same shape as all the others — same command, same `agent:branch:message` form, nothing private.
`commitTheTask` ends by asking `git diff --cached --quiet` whether anything is *still* staged: staged work left
behind means the commit did not take, and that is what `reason=commit-failed` means. The hook then pushes for itself: cheap when the branch is current, and it
is the hook's own guarantee that the branch is on the forge before a PR is opened off it — which is why
`reason=push-failed` is a separate alarm from `reason=commit-failed`. `sf` is in `requireTools` for this
reason; `reason=missing-tool tool=sf` is the alarm when the CLI is not installed. Both of those calls are made
inside the tower's own folders, and the loop only ever runs a tower from inside the factory, so the check on the
`sf` node is met.

**One branch, one pull request, forever:** the branch's first task creates the PR, and every later task finds
it by `gh pr list --head <branch> --base <target> --state open` and reuses it — which is why the branch, not
the ticket, is the identity of the work.

**Two paths, decided by one key.** Everything above the `SF_WORK_KIND` test is shared: guard, branch,
commit, push, forge. A claim whose `kind=gate` (read off the dispatched claims line, falling back to the
context file, defaulting to `task`) then takes a different road: it looks the branch's PR up **at any state**
— `gh pr list … --state all` / `glab mr list … --all`, not `--state open` — because the lead agent has just
merged it and an open-only lookup would find nothing. With no PR at all the hook raises
`reason=pr-not-found` and leaves the gate claimed: there was nothing there to merge. With one, it annotates
the gate with the url, closes the **gate** as its claimant, writes `closes url=… gate=true`, removes the
context file and exits. **It never opens a new PR on the gate path** — the lead agent merged the branch
through the forge, and a second PR off a merged branch is a lie about work nobody did.
"""
    link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/src/agent-hooks/worker-stop.sh "Source — CLI/src/agent-hooks/worker-stop.sh"
  }

  overseerAgent = worker "overseer — the reader, never the hand" {
    summary "`sf overseer`: reads this directory's tower and its run logs and reports back to you, foreground. `sf` is denied to it outright."
    description """
**What it is:** `.claude/agents/overseer.md`, run in the **foreground** by `sf overseer` →
`CLI/src/overseer-agent.sh`, which execs `$SF_AGENT_LAUNCH_COMMAND` — your `SF_AGENT_COMMAND` plus the flags every
spawn carries, since it too stands in a tower and not in the factory — with the `overseer` agent,
`--disallowedTools 'Bash(sf:*)'`, and a short **launch brief prepended to your prompt**. The `PATH` guard below still checks the plain `SF_AGENT_COMMAND`. Foreground is
the whole difference from a worker: a worker is spawned `--bg`
with nobody watching and reports through the log; the overseer talks back to you, and anything you type after
the command goes to it as the prompt — after that brief, which it cannot lose.

**Two of its facts come from that brief, never from its own environment.** The engine bakes the **log root** and
the **agent command** into the prompt it hands over — `overseer-agent.sh` interpolates them off its own
`SF_LOG_ROOT` and `SF_AGENT_COMMAND` — and `overseer.md` writes them as `<log root>` and `<agent command>`
throughout. The watcher is a session like any other: whatever its shell happens to carry about a run can belong to
a different one.

**It reads three things and mutates nothing:** the beads database of the directory it was started in, the run
logs under `<log root>`, and git. It knows how a tower got where it is — what each plan agent writes, tier by
tier, what a gate is, what should be running next — which is what lets it tell a real fault from a tier simply
waiting on a human.

| Guard | What it stops |
|---|---|
| `sf` denied at the command level | `sf commit`, `sf loop`, `sf plan:*` — while `bd`, `git` and every other read still run |
| no `Write`, `Edit` or `Agent` tools in its own file | writing a file, or spawning a worker of its own |
| `requireBeadsProject`, then the first word of `SF_AGENT_COMMAND` on `PATH` | running it outside a tower, or with an agent command that does not exist |

**Where the fix is a mutation, it hands you the command to run.** That is the design and not a limitation:
claim, unclaim, close, commit and push belong to the loop and its hooks, and a reader that could do them
itself would be a second loop nobody dispatched.
"""
    link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/.claude/agents/overseer.md "Source — .claude/agents/overseer.md"
    link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/src/overseer-agent.sh "Source — CLI/src/overseer-agent.sh"
  }

  beads = beadsStore "beads DB (bd) — the tower" {
    summary "The database holding every gate, ticket and edge — the loop's only runtime input."
    description """
**What it is:** the loop's **only runtime input**. None of the design docs or CLAUDE.md files are read at
runtime; the graph — every gate, ticket, `blocks` edge, and the metadata keys — lives here. The commands
that read and write it are in the toolbelt; what lives here is the **state**, and the state is two keys.

**The two metadata keys, and only two.** One is the plan's, one is the loop's or the hook's:

| Fact | Where it lives | Who reads it |
|---|---|---|
| `branch` | on a **gate only** — the epic gate and each feature gate carry their own, written once by the `plan-*` agent that owns the tier. No task, suite or review carries one | the loop, through `readFieldOf <gate> branch`, on the gate the ticket's id names — then written into the dispatch record, the claims line's `branch=` with the context file's `branch=` beside it, which is where the Stop hook reads it back; nothing parses text for it |
| `pr` | the url, written by the loop (onto the epic gate) or the Stop hook (onto any ticket whose work landed) — never by a plan agent | a human, and the hook when it looks for the PR already on a branch |

**Everything else about a ticket's git world is derived, never stored.** An id reads
`<repo>-<kind>-<uuid>-<hierarchy>`; for `box-task-7f3a9c-1.3` the uuid names its tower, the kind word
swapped to `feature` with the hierarchy cut at the last dot names the feature gate whose `branch` the work
lands on, and the kind word swapped to `epic` with hierarchy `0` names the epic gate whose `branch` is the
**`prTarget`** — `main` when the owner is the epic gate itself. Which tier a gate is is the kind word of its
own id, `epic` or `feature` — nothing is read from the database to work it out. A stored copy of either is a
fact that can disagree with the gate that decides it, which is why neither is a key.

**There is no `parent` key, no `parent-child` edge, and no `--parent` at create time.** Parenthood is what
made a gate unenforceable: beads refuses a `blocks` edge from an ancestor to its own descendant. With nothing
to walk, the id is the only thing pointing at a gate — so every ticket of a tower is created with an explicit
`--id`, and a flat one reaches no gate: the loop refuses it, `ERROR reason=no-epic-gate ticket=<id>`, and
leaves it claimed on purpose. Every id comes out of `CLI/src/create-beads/create-gate.sh` or
`CLI/src/create-beads/create-task.sh` — both in the **factory checkout**, not in the tower a plan agent stands in
— and never from an eye. A gate is a **custom type** and must be registered
(`bd config set types.custom \"gate\"`), as is the parking status
(`bd config set status.custom \"failed-agent:frozen\"`): `bd` does not reject an unregistered type or status, it
**accepts it silently and then refuses it**, so a broken gate and a working one look identical. Check
`bd types` before you trust one.
"""
    link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/.claude/skills/bead-initialize/SKILL.md "Source — the bead-initialize skill"
  }

  forge = forge "origin — GitHub or GitLab" {
    summary "The remote the tower publishes to: one pull request per branch."
    description """
**What it is:** the forge the tower publishes to. `main` at the bottom, one PR per branch above it:
`feature/<slug>` → PR → the epic branch, `epic/<slug>` → PR → `main`.

**Which forge it is, is settled three ways, in this order** (`detectForge` in `CLI/utils/forge.sh`, used by both the
loop and the Stop hook): an honest host name in the remote URL (`*gitlab*`, `*github*`); then the refs that host
actually publishes, because only GitHub serves `refs/pull/` and only GitLab serves
`refs/merge-requests|pipelines|environments/`; then a machine holding **exactly one** of `gh`/`glab`.
Where all three are ambiguous the hook refuses to guess and logs `forge-unknown` — a wrong guess pushes
a branch to the wrong forge.
"""
    link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/CLI/utils/forge.sh "Source — CLI/utils/forge.sh"
  }

  toolbelt = layer "The commands the loop shells out to" {
    summary "The commands the loop shells out to: the ticket tool, the agent command, git, the forge tool, the folder."

    bdCmds = noteDoc "bd — the only reads and writes the tower answers to" {
      summary "The ticket commands the tower answers to, and why each one is that one."
      description """
| Command | Why this one |
|---|---|
| `bd ready --claim --json` | the **only** sanctioned claim: atomic assignee + `in_progress` + 5-minute lease |
| `bd ready --json` (peek) | used once, inside `waiting()`, purely to name `next=` |
| `bd unclaim <id>` | put a claim back — the two places `releaseClaim` is called, listed on the claim node |
| `bd heartbeat <id>` | the worker keeps its lease alive; it never closes its own ticket |
| `bd list --status in_progress --limit 50 --json \| jq length` | a **count**, taken only once the capacity test has already said the run is full: `0` makes the waiting line `max_parallel_stalled`, anything else makes it `max_parallel in_progress=N`. It is never a listing, and nothing walks it — the loop runs no busy-branch check |
| `bd list --status open,blocked --limit 0 --json` | the probe that tells `frontier_empty` from `tower_stalled` |
| `bd show --json <id>` | read one ticket back — its status, its title (`--long`, the commit message), its `issue_type` (is this row a gate?), and a gate's `branch` |
| `bd update <id> --set-metadata k=v` | **merges** — which is why writing `pr` never disturbs the plan's `branch` |
| `bd update <id> --status <s> --if-status <from>` | **the gate claim**, and the only conditional write in the system: `moveGateTo <gate> in_progress open` is the take, `moveGateTo <gate> open in_progress` is the put-back, and a non-zero exit (the gate was no longer in `<from>`) is the `gate-claim-lost` alarm. A gate never reaches `bd ready`, so this is the only way it moves |
| `bd close <id> --session <id> --reason …` | only the Stop hook, only once this session's dispatch is on the `claims` line — a task or a gate, never by the worker itself |
| `bd list --include-gates --ready --type gate --limit 0 --json` | the ready gates, `.id` alone — the only listing that shows a gate at all, and the input to `dispatchReadyGates`. The tier is not on the row; `gateTierOf` reads it off the id's kind word |
| `CLI/src/create-beads/create-gate.sh <repo> [<epicGateId>]` then `bd create -t gate --id <what it printed> --metadata '{"branch":…}'` | a plan agent mints a gate id and nothing else: no gate id is ever typed. With an epic gate id in hand it numbers the next feature, with none it mints the epic and its one uuid |
| `CLI/src/create-beads/create-task.sh <repo> <gateId> [suite\|review]` then `bd create -t task -l <label> --id <what it printed>` | a plan agent mints a task id and nothing else: a gate carries **zero** labels and the `branch` key alone, a task exactly one dispatching label and **no metadata at all**. `.1` and `.2` are fixed to a gate's suite and review, and asking for a second one refuses; above `.2` it takes
the highest of that gate's existing task ids and adds one — counted off the database, not off the graph, so a
ticket whose edge was never written still holds its number. Every ticket of a tower carries an explicit `--id` — a flat one reaches no gate. There is **no `--parent`** anywhere in a tower. Both scripts live in the **factory checkout**, which is one of a plan agent's working directories — they are not inside the tower it stands in, and its cwd stays there, because cwd is what decides which beads database `bd` reads and therefore which tower gets counted |
| `bd dep add <blocked> <blocker> --type blocks` | the **one edge type in a tower**, read "blocker blocks blocked"; what a tier *is* = the tickets that block its gate |
| `bd where` | which DB a directory resolves — `bd` walks **up** and ignores `BEADS_DIR` |

A gate is a **custom type** and must be registered (`bd config set types.custom \"gate\"`): `bd` does not reject
an unregistered type, it **accepts and hides it**, so a broken gate and a working one look identical. Check
`bd types` before you trust one.
"""
    }

    claudeCli = cliTool "$SF_AGENT_COMMAND (claude) — the command every agent runs through" {
      summary "The command every agent runs through, and the launch form composed off it for every spawn — which of the two a call uses is not interchangeable."
      description """
`SF_AGENT_COMMAND`, default `claude` — the one command every agent in the system runs through, so
pointing it at your own build moves all of them. `loadSpiderFactoryEnv` composes a second name off it,
`SF_AGENT_LAUNCH_COMMAND` = `$SF_AGENT_COMMAND --add-dir <factory root>`, and **which of the two a call uses is
not interchangeable**: the three sites that have to *resolve an `--agent`* — the worker spawn, the three planners,
the watcher — take the launch form, and every other call stays on the plain name.

- `$SF_AGENT_LAUNCH_COMMAND --bg --agent <agent> --permission-mode bypassPermissions \"<the brief>\"` —
  the worker spawn, run with `cd` **inside** the branch's worktree, and deliberately **no `-w`** —
  `claude --worktree` only ever *creates* one, and this branch already owns its own. The lead agent's gate spawn
  is the same call with `--agent lead`, and the brief is the same `buildWorkerPrompt` with one branch of one
  variable: `$stayOnBranchRule` is rule 4, chosen by `[[ "$agentName" == "$leadAgentName" ]]`, and the lead variant
  is the only text that names the target or says `sf commit` outright. Nothing else is added, so a gate session is
  handed nothing a worker is not. What that brief actually says is on the workers layer.
- `$SF_AGENT_LAUNCH_COMMAND --agent <planner> \"<prompt>\"` — the three plan agents, foreground, through
  `CLI/src/plan-agent.sh`, which is what `sf plan:epic`, `sf plan:feature` and `sf plan:task` run.
- `$SF_AGENT_LAUNCH_COMMAND --agent overseer --disallowedTools 'Bash(sf:*)' \"<launch brief> <your prompt>\"` — the
  reader, foreground, through `CLI/src/overseer-agent.sh`. The engine prepends a one-sentence brief naming the run
  logs' directory and the command that lists background sessions, both interpolated off the engine's own
  `SF_LOG_ROOT` and `SF_AGENT_COMMAND`, and tells the watcher to take those two from the brief and never off its own
  environment. It is the only spawn that hands a session a fact this way; the worker's facts ride in its prompt and
  the dispatch record.
- `$SF_AGENT_COMMAND agents --json` — the capacity count, recounted fresh every tick: background
  sessions reporting `status: busy` whose `cwd` starts with `<tower>/.claude/worktrees` or equals the tower.
  All three tests are load-bearing. A finished `--bg` session is never removed from this listing — it stays
  `kind: background` and only its status drops to `idle` — so without the status test a worker that finished
  would hold a slot for the rest of the run. Nothing here ever stops a session: a finished worker is left
  running on purpose, so it stays readable in the agent listing.
- bare `sf` — `exec ${SF_AGENT_COMMAND:-claude}`, no `--add-dir`: that session `cd`s to the factory root itself,
  so the agents and skills are already under its project root.

**Why the launch form adds a directory.** A worker's cwd is a git worktree, and the planners and the overseer run
inside a tower project — so in every one of those sessions the project root is the tower, never the factory, and
Claude Code discovers `.claude/agents/` and `.claude/skills/` only from a session's own project roots. Without the
added directory `--agent <name>` resolves to nothing and no skill loads: the spawn comes back failed as
`worker-not-dispatched`, and a planner cannot run at all. `--add-dir` makes `spider-factory/` a **second project
root for discovery only** — it is **not a sandbox and not a permission grant**; it changes nothing about where the
session works or what it may touch. The root is derived from `CLI/utils/user-env.sh`'s own location, so it always
names the checkout the code is actually running from.

`SF_AGENT_COMMAND` is split on spaces, so it may carry its own arguments, and its first word is what the PATH checks
look for. **Every place the plain name survives is a read, never a spawn:** `beads.sh`'s `agents --json` (the
`--add-dir` flag is variadic and would swallow the `agents` positional as a second directory), the loop's startup
`requireToolsOnPath`, the `requireAgentCommand` each of the two foreground engines runs before it execs, and bare
`sf`'s own `exec`.

Workers run `bypassPermissions` unattended, so this loop only ever belongs in a repo you fully trust.
"""
    }

    gitCli = cliTool "git — worktrees, fetch, push" {
      summary "The git the loop runs: is a branch checked out, make its folder, fetch, push."
      description """
The loop owns the folders, because a worktree belongs to a **branch**, not to a ticket.

- `branchAlreadyCheckedOut`: parse `git worktree list --porcelain` for `refs/heads/<branch>` — a branch
  already checked out somewhere is reused, never re-added.
- `ensureWorktree <branch> <prTarget>` → `git worktree add -B <branch> <path> <start>`, under
  `<tower>/.claude/worktrees/`, where the folder name is the branch name with `/` → `+`
  (`feature/add-record` → `feature+add-record`).
- `<start>` is the **first** of `origin/<branch>`, `origin/<prTarget>`, `<prTarget>`, `HEAD` that resolves —
  a feature branch starts where it is going to land.
- `git -C <tower> fetch --quiet origin` before each lookup, and the loop's one push. When the branch does not
  exist on the remote yet, or sits exactly on its target, `ensureBranchAheadOfTarget` first writes it a commit of
  its own — `git -C <tower> commit-tree <target>^{tree} -p <target> -m \"chore: open <branch>\"` — then runs
  `git -C <tower> push --quiet origin <seedSha>:refs/heads/<branch>`, publishing an epic branch that has none.
  The tower's own working tree is never touched and its HEAD never moves.
- **Nothing else here commits.** `git add -A`, `git commit -m \"agent:branch:message\"` and
  `git push -u origin <branch>` all live inside `sf commit` (`CLI/src/commit.sh`) — the only way anything
  commits in this repo. The agent is the one the loop dispatched onto **this folder**: `commit.sh` loads the
  environment only to find the log root, then reads the `agent=` key of that folder's dispatch context file,
  `<log root>/worker-context/<worktree folder name>` — so the name is a fact about the folder, not about the shell
  that typed the command, and no dispatch record means `manual`, a person standing in a terminal. The branch is
  `git symbolic-ref --short HEAD`, and a detached HEAD is refused because a branch it cannot name is a
  subject it cannot write.
"""
    }

    forgeCli = cliTool "gh / glab" {
      summary "gh or glab, whichever the remote settled on: find the branch's pull request, open one if none."
      description """
Whichever `detectForge` settled on. Both are used the same way: list the PR already on this branch pair,
and create one only when there is none. Only a URL matching `/(pull|merge_requests)/[0-9]+` counts as a
pull request — an error message quoting a docs URL is not one.
"""
    }

    worktree = branchBox "the branch's folder — .claude/worktrees/<branch with / → +>" {
      summary "One folder per branch, where every ticket of that feature works in turn."
      description """
One folder per **branch**, not per ticket, which is why the loop owns it: `feature/add-record` lives at
`.claude/worktrees/feature+add-record`, and every ticket of that feature works in the same folder, one after
the other, each one's commit landing on the last.

```
git worktree list --porcelain          is this branch checked out somewhere already? reuse it
git worktree add -B <branch> <path> <start>
<start> = origin/<branch> | origin/<prTarget> | <prTarget> | HEAD   — the first that resolves
git -C <tower> fetch --quiet origin    before each lookup
```

A feature branch starts where it is going to land. The worker is spawned with its cwd **inside** this folder,
and it lands its own finished pieces here as it goes — `sf commit`, one commit per piece (the rule is on the
workers layer) — never a bare `git commit` or `git push`, never a branch of its own, so two hands never write
one branch. The tower root (where `.beads` lives) is never inherited from this folder either: it reaches the worker
as a literal path in its prompt and sits in the dispatch record as `tower=`. **The worktree is not the tower.**
"""
    }

  }  // toolbelt

  human = human "Human (Brodie)" {
    summary "Closes the gates and nothing else — until a tier's flag hands that gate to the lead agent."
    description """
**What it is:** the one who closes a **gate**, unless that tier's switch says otherwise. A *task* is closed by
the Stop hook, when its worker's session ends — the human never reaches for a task, and a worker that
closes its own ticket is a bug, because the hook walks past an already-closed ticket and lands nothing. The
human reads the worker's report — which is the comment the worker left on its own ticket, since the final message
it was typed into died with that worker's session — verifies the branch and the PR, and closes the gate above it.
There is **no `human` agent** — the person enters through gates, and nothing is ever spawned for them. A claimable ticket
assigned to a human is a model bug.

Both gates are the human's by default: `SF_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES=0` and `SF_AGENT_EPIC_GATES=0`, and a gate stays
open until someone verifies it. Setting a tier's flag to `1` hands *that* tier to the **lead agent**, which the
loop spawns for a ready gate and whose Stop hook closes it — the other tier is still the human's, which is why
there are two switches and not one. The two are not the same amount of trust: a feature gate merges one flow
into the epic branch, the epic gate merges the whole thing into `main`.

`bd ready` never shows a gate, so a tower **stalls** at an open one until it is verified — by a person, or by
the lead agent proving the tier out loud. That is the design, not a bug — it is the only place a claim loop
cannot fake understanding.
"""
  }

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE WORKERS — the spokes. Each is one agent file, dispatched by one label.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  workers = layer "The workers" {
    summary "The seven workers: six are reached by a label, the seventh — the lead — only by a gate."
    description """
**Every worker reports on its own ticket before it stops.** All seven agent files carry the same section, `## Report
On The Ticket`, word for word identical across the seven: before it stops, the worker puts its **result** on its own
ticket through the `bead-comment` skill — what it did, and when the brief outran it, exactly what is left. The
section owns only the fact that it happens; what belongs on a ticket and how a comment reads is the skill's, and the
section deliberately restates none of those rules. This is why the section exists: the final message dies with the
session, so the ticket — with the branch and its pull request — is what the next reader actually has. The ticket is a
third surface beside the final message and the pull request, and the only one of the three that carries the worker's
own account of the whole brief. Six of the seven preload `bead-read` and `bead-comment` in their `skills:`
frontmatter; `review` preloads nothing and loads both at its Step 1, and it is the one worker with **two** comment
surfaces — its own ticket, and the pull request its findings go on.

**The contract is in the agent file.** Every worker but the lead carries a `## The contract` section, and each of
those six runs **four** bullets, all of them beside the standing instruction to keep the five-minute lease alive
with `bd heartbeat <ticket>` between long steps: never close your own ticket (the Stop hook
closes it when your turn ends, and a ticket already closed makes the hook walk past it, landing nothing); **report
on your own ticket**, one line pointing at the `## Report On The Ticket` section below it, because that section is
what the next reader gets of the work; spawn nothing; and **finish the work before you stop** — stopping is what
finishes the ticket, so stop only when you would hand the brief to a human as it stands. The comment duty is a named
section, not a bullet: the bullet only points at it, and the section itself restates none of the skill's rules.
There is no declaration of completion to make and nothing to touch at the end: the session ending *is* the
hand-off, which is why the contract spends its last bullet on when it is honest to stop rather than on how to
signal that you are done.
**Six of the seven say spawn nothing** — `engineer`, `frontend` and the three testers each carry that bullet
verbatim, and the lead carries it as hard rule 6. **`review` is the one
exception**: its own contract says *spawn fixers, once each, one at a time, and file no tickets*, and it is
the only agent file in the tower whose `tools:` line carries `Agent`. The round is on its node. The `lead`
agent states the same rules inside its own steps and hard rules rather than in a section of that name — `bd
heartbeat <gate>`, never `bd close` not even its own, spawn nothing, and never invent a branch, a target, a test
command, or a ticket; it carries the report section too, with no bullet pointing at it, having no contract section
to hold one. No skill holds these rules, and the agent file is the **only** place most of them are written at all — the brief the loop
sends is not a copy of this contract.

**What the loop's brief actually sends.** `buildWorkerPrompt` (`CLI/src/beads-loop/worker.sh`) builds the one
argument the spawn passes after the flags, and it is short: the ticket id, a `FIRST:` line ordering the worker to
read `bd show --json <ticket>` in full as its complete brief, one paragraph saying this folder is the worktree of
a branch shared by every task of the feature so earlier commits may already be sitting there, and then exactly
**four** numbered rules, running **1, 2, 3, 4** with no gaps. Every fact in it is a **literal** interpolated into
that one `printf` — the ticket id, the branch, the tower's path, and for the lead agent its `prTarget` — because the
spawn hands the session no environment to read any of them off. A worker's brief never names the pull request's
target: `prTarget` is interpolated only into the lead variant of rule 4. The four are: ground every beads command before you run it; keep
the five-minute lease alive with `bd heartbeat <ticket>`; never run `bd close`,
because the Stop hook closes the ticket when your turn ends and closing it by hand makes the hook walk past it —
that same rule 3 is where the brief says *finish the work before you stop*, because stopping is what finishes the
ticket;
and stay on `${branch}`, in this folder — never create a branch, never check one out, never merge, never rebase,
never a bare `git commit` or `git push`, and commit as your agent file tells you as you go, because whatever is
still uncommitted when you stop becomes one last commit on the branch carrying your ticket's title.
**Rule 1 is the one that guards the database**, and it is there because `bd` finds its database by walking **up**
from wherever the session happens to be standing: the rule names the tower's path **as a literal** — the same path
the dispatch record carries under `tower=` — and orders the worker to run `bd where` and make it name that database.
When it names another one, the remedy the rule gives is `-C '<tower>'` on every beads command from then on, said out
loud in the report; **never** `cd` into a tower that is not its own. One stray `cd` and the
worker is reading somebody else's tower, claiming and closing tickets in a tower it was never dispatched into.
Rule 4 is the only part that differs by agent — for the
**lead** it says this ticket is a GATE, that you verify the tier it stands for and then merge this branch's own
pull request into its target with the forge's own command (`gh pr merge` / `glab mr merge`) and never a local
`git merge` into the target — built as `$stayOnBranchRule` and interpolated into the one `printf`, which is
closed and parses. What the brief still never says: the report-on-the-ticket section — no line of it orders the
worker to comment its result on its ticket — and any ban on spawning; so the
prompt is no safety net for either of them, and both live only in the worker's own agent file.

**The same file holds the hook registration.** Each of these seven agent files carries a `hooks:` block in its own
YAML frontmatter binding `Stop` → `sf hook stop` (timeout 300) — that block, and nothing
else, is what makes a worker's session ending finish the work. It is the only event any of them binds: there is no
`StopFailure` event to bind, and nothing runs when a session dies on an error — see the run log's own node for
what a dead worker leaves behind, which is nothing in any file.
The overseer and the three plan agents carry no `hooks:` block at all,
so their sessions fire no hook; the mechanism is on the Stop hook's node.

**The five that build, commit as they go.** `engineer`, `frontend`, `tester-unit`, `tester-feature` and
`tester-e2e` each carry a `## Commit As You Go` section running a bare `sf commit <what you just did>` after every
major change that stands on its own — one commit per piece, so the pull request shows the work as it happens
instead of one blob at the end. **Nothing prefixes that command with an agent name**, and no agent file does: the
name in the `agent:branch:message` subject is read off the dispatch record for the folder the commit stands in, not
out of the shell that typed it — which is exactly why the command is bare. `lead` and `review` have no such section: `review` commits
nothing at all — its fixes reach the branch only through its fixers' own `sf commit`, never its own — and the
lead commits only what it had to resolve where the target moved under the branch.
"""
    engineer = worker "engineer" {
      summary "Business logic, endpoints, hooks, types, error factories; never tests, JSX or CSS."
      description """
**Label `backend-logic` → `engineer`.** Business logic, endpoints, hooks, types, error factories.
Never writes tests, JSX or CSS. Preloaded skills: `bead-read`, `bead-comment` and the four standards; the worker
contract, and the `## Report On The Ticket` section that files its result on the ticket, both live in the agent file
itself.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/.claude/agents/engineer.md "Source — .claude/agents/engineer.md"
    }
    testerUnit = worker "tester-unit" {
      summary "Micro tier: one back-end function alone — every branch, boundary and error it throws."
      description """
**Label `unit-tests` → `tester-unit` — the micro tier.** One back-end function alone; every branch, every
boundary, empty/null, every error it throws. Never a component: the UI is proved by the composition tier.
Waits on the back-end ticket it tests. Skill: `framework-jest`.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/.claude/agents/tester-unit.md "Source — .claude/agents/tester-unit.md"
    }
    frontend = worker "frontend" {
      summary "Components, layout and styling, last in the line against functions already proved."
      description """
**Label `frontend-ui` → `frontend`.** Components, layout, styling, the shared component system. Last in the
line, built against functions already proved. Its landing is what unblocks `feature-test`.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/.claude/agents/frontend.md "Source — .claude/agents/frontend.md"
    }
    testerFeature = worker "tester-feature" {
      summary "Composition tier: one feature's pieces proved wired, its errors and successes surfacing."
      description """
**Label `feature-test` → `tester-feature` — the composition tier.** One feature's pieces proved wired; its
errors and successes surface. Never re-runs the micro tier's edge matrix, never walks cross-feature journeys.
Skill: `framework-playwright`.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/.claude/agents/tester-feature.md "Source — .claude/agents/tester-feature.md"
    }
    testerE2e = worker "tester-e2e" {
      summary "Broad tier: whole user journeys across features in a real browser on the real stack."
      description """
**Label `epic-e2e-test` → `tester-e2e` — the broad tier.** Journeys across features on the real stack:
cross-feature state handoffs, session continuity, one success and one failure journey per feature. The least
verbose tier. Skill: `framework-playwright`.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/.claude/agents/tester-e2e.md "Source — .claude/agents/tester-e2e.md"
    }
    reviewAgent = worker "review" {
      summary "Three questions as PR comments, then every finding handed one at a time to the agent that owns its fix."
      description """
**Labels `feature-review` and `epic-review` → `review`.** Three questions from first principles: does the code
do what its source of truth demands, do the tests prove it, is it simple and readable. Maps the system the
change sits in (entry → exit, following imports), instruments it with trace logs, runs the tests against the
instrumented code, posts **each finding as its own PR comment**, pinned to the line it belongs to and complete
enough to fix from, then one summary comment with the whole picture. Files no tickets, **creates no files at
all** — not a findings file, not a report, not notes, nothing new ever appears in that worktree — and commits or
pushes nothing: its **findings** live on the pull request and in its final message and nowhere else — never on its
own ticket, which is a different surface and carries something else, its result, the way every other worker's does.
The only writes it makes are the trace logs of Step 4, into files that already exist, sitting in its own working tree;
`sf commit` runs `git add -A`, so anything left there rides onto the branch it is judging, which is why it
**strips them back out before it stops** and checks with `git status` that the tree holds nothing at all.
Nothing is preloaded — `bead-read` and `bead-comment` together at Step 1, the four standards at Step 6, and
`pr-comments` at Step 7 all load on demand at the step that uses them. Step 1 loads `bead-comment` for exactly one reason, and says so: it is how review writes on **its
own** ticket, which is not the surface its findings go on.

**Its branch is the folder it stands on; its base is read through `bd`.** The loop checked the branch out and put
the session inside it, so `git branch --show-current` is the whole answer and the only thing it trusts — never
anything its own shell carries about a dispatch: `claude --bg` runs it through the shared daemon that froze the
environment at the run's first spawn, so whatever is in there can be another worker's branch entirely. It never reviews a branch against itself: it diffs against the **epic gate's** `branch` metadata, read
off `<repo>-epic-<uuid>-0` with `bd show --json`, and against `main` when its own hierarchy starts at `0`.
**An empty `git diff --stat` is a finding, never a pass** — either the base resolved wrong or the branch holds
nothing; it says which, and stops rather than reviewing an empty diff.

**Step 8 — one round of fixers, then it stops looking.** Once the findings are posted and the instrumentation
stripped, review spawns **one fixer per finding, one at a time, in sequence, one round only**: it spawns the
first, waits for it to report, then spawns the next, and never puts two of them on the branch together — they
share one working tree and one branch, and two fixers editing the same files step on each other's commits. It
routes by **what the fix has to touch** — never by how severe the finding is nor by which of the three questions
raised it: `engineer` for logic, data, a hook, an endpoint, an error path or a standard violated in non-UI code;
`frontend` for a component, JSX, layout, styling, or anything the user sees; and for behaviour the tests could
not have failed on, the test tier matched to the **depth of the gap** — unit for one back-end function alone,
feature for one feature's pieces wired, e2e for a journey across features — going one tier down when the depth
is unclear. A finding needing both a change and its proof is **two spawns**, because a fixer never writes what
another tier owns; they still go one after the other. These are nested sessions inside review's own — it is the
only agent file in the tower whose `tools:` line carries `Agent` — and each arrives knowing nothing, so **the
finding's own PR comment is the whole brief**: review pastes that comment body verbatim into the prompt and adds
nothing it does not already say, putting alongside it only the url of the comment it came from, the branch it is
standing on, and the name of the review ticket, for context. A comment that is not enough to fix from was never
a finished finding: the missing detail goes back onto the pull request before the spawn.

**Every prompt ends with the same three constraints:** fix this finding and nothing beside it; commit it
yourself with `sf commit`; and touch no ticket — no `bd close`, no `bd create`, no comment on anything. A fixer
has no ticket of its own, so the `## Report On The Ticket` section its own agent file carries **stays shut** —
the fix it commits is its report, and the hook finds no `reason=dispatched` line for a session the loop never
spawned, so it closes nothing. **They
commit; review commits nothing.** A fixer's `sf commit` is what lands a fix on the branch; review never runs
`git add`, `git commit`, or `git push`, and never fixes what a fixer left broken. Each fixer is a session of its
own, and each one's own `Stop` event is what finishes it — review's turn ends only when review itself stops, so a
fixer reporting back can never close the review ticket out from under it. Then review re-runs no step, reads no
fixer's diff, and spawns no second round.

**Then it closes out its own threads.** Once the last fixer has reported, review takes each finding back to the
thread it posted it in and resolves it — the ones whose fix landed, and only those. The resolve calls live in
this agent's own file, not in `pr-comments`, which is posting-only and owns no resolve verb: GitHub resolves a
**thread**, so it reads the thread ids off `gh api graphql … reviewThreads` first and only then fires
`resolveReviewThread`; GitLab resolves a **discussion** by its id with
`glab api --method PUT …/discussions/<id> -f resolved=true`. A finding whose fixer came back wrong, or came back
saying it was not a defect, stays **open**: an open thread is the human's signal that this one still needs a
person. It never resolves a comment it did not post, never approves, never merges — approve and merge are the
human's, and are what the `lead` agent and the person closing a gate do, not this worker.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/.claude/agents/review.md "Source — .claude/agents/review.md"
    }
    leadAgent = worker "lead" {
      summary "The one worker no label reaches: stands on a gate, proves the tier, merges the branch through the forge."
      description """
**No label reaches this agent — a gate does.** `leadAgentName=\"lead\"` sits beside the `AGENT` map, not inside
it: the loop spawns the lead only from `dispatchOneGate`, for a ready gate whose tier flag is on, with the
identity `agent=lead label=gate:<tier>`. Everything below it has already been committed, pushed, reviewed and
closed; its whole job is to find out whether any of that is a lie.

**A closed ticket is a claim, not a fact.** Its proof is never the ticket list — it is four checks, each read
out with its numbers: every ticket that blocks the gate is closed; this tier's review actually reviewed and
every finding is answered on the branch; the project's own test command exits `0` **with a count above zero**
(the epic tier runs the end-to-end command too); the pull request's pipeline is green, where queued and skipped
are not green. Any one of them failing means it says *this gate is not proved*, comments the gate naming what is
missing, and stops there — the gate stays claimed and a human is told why.

**Its report goes on the gate, not only in its message.** Step 8 is one line per ticket in the tier, the test
command with its count, each pipeline check, each review finding and how it was answered, and the merge with its
result — and the `## Report On The Ticket` section the lead carries like every other worker makes it file that same
report on its own gate through `bead-comment` before it stops, because its final message dies with its session and
the gate is the only thing the person closing it will still be able to read. Preloaded skills: `bead-read` and
`bead-comment`.

**Merge, and only now** — `gh pr merge` / `glab mr merge` on that branch's PR into its **`prTarget`**, which
Step 1 derived off the gate's own id: the epic gate's `branch`, or `main` when the gate is the epic gate. The
forge does the merge: never a local `git merge` into the target, never a push to the target, never a rebase.
Where the target moved under the branch it brings the target *into this branch*, with one
command and no others: `git -c merge.conflictStyle=diff3 merge origin/<prTarget>`. The style is `merge` unless it is
asked for, and a plain `git merge` then hands over two sides with no `|||||||` base section showing what the code
looked like before either side touched it — which is the only legitimate way to choose between two sides. The setting
is asked for **on the command line and never written into anyone's config**. A test file in that conflict is spliced,
never chosen: union of the cases, both sides kept, and where both assert they are made to agree by fixing the code,
never by silencing an assertion — a test weakened to end a conflict makes everything above the gate a lie.
It
commits the resolution with `sf commit` and pushes, so the PR carries it and the pipeline re-runs. Writes no
code and no tests; never closes, reopens, unclaims or reclaims a ticket — `bd close` would end the hook before
it ever ran.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/.claude/agents/lead.md "Source — .claude/agents/lead.md"
    }
  }

  planAgents = layer "The plan agents (outside the tower)" {
    summary "The judgments the machine never makes: what the epic is, what a feature is, the order in one."
    description """
The judgments the machine never makes belong here: **what the epic is** (one `epic/<slug>` of two or three
lowercase words saying what the thing *is*); **what counts as a feature** (a complete user-facing flow, phased
foundations → core → dependent → polish); **the order inside a feature's line** (the logic, then its tests,
then the UI — never a fan); **the tier of a test ticket** (the label carries the tier, never `--agent`). Each
plan agent writes **one** metadata key and only one: `branch`, and only ever onto **its own gate**. The epic
planner names `epic/<slug>` on the epic gate, the feature planner names `feature/<slug>` on its own — and
nothing below a gate carries a copy of either, because a copy is a fact that can disagree with the gate that
decides it. The other half of what a plan agent writes is the **id**: every ticket of a tower is created with
an explicit `--id`, because that id is the only thing the loop reads to find the two gates above it — and no
plan agent ever numbers one itself, it runs `CLI/src/create-beads/create-gate.sh` or `create-task.sh` and passes
what comes back. Nothing below recomputes a branch, and there is no parent chain to walk.

**Where a plan agent stands, and what it can therefore reach.** Each of these sessions runs **inside the tower
project**, not in the factory: `--add-dir` makes the factory checkout a second project root for discovery only,
which is how its own agent file, its skills, the two id generators and the `plan-tasks` templates are readable at
all. Name a factory file by the checkout; never assume the cwd holds it. The cwd itself stays in the tower on
purpose — it is the cwd that decides which beads database `bd` reads, and therefore which tower gets counted.

**Their `tools:` lines are three different lists, and one of them is not a typo.** `plan-epic` alone carries
`Write`, `Edit`, the four task tools and the web tools (`WebSearch`, `WebFetch`, `mcp__searxng__*`) — it is the one
planner that has to research a vague idea and write down what it found. `plan-feature` and `plan-task` carry
`Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, Skill` and nothing else: they read the tier above, then write tickets through `bd` and
write no file anywhere. Never assume the three are symmetric — a step, a brief or a fix aimed at "the planners"
breaks on whichever of them lacks the tool.

Each is one foreground command — `sf plan:epic`, `sf plan:feature`, `sf plan:task` — and anything you type
after it goes to the planner as the prompt. What runs them is on the agent-command node.
"""
    planEpic = planner "plan-epic" {
      summary "A vague idea into exactly one epic gate plus its end-to-end and review tickets."
      description """
Vague idea → clarifying questions → research → **exactly one** epic gate plus its `epic-e2e-test` and
`epic-review` tickets, and nothing else: **there is no merge ticket** and no label for one, because the lead
agent merges from the gate. Its gate's id comes from `create-gate.sh <repo>` — a fresh uuid and hierarchy `0`;
it writes one key, `branch: epic/<slug>`, onto the gate alone; its two tickets
carry no metadata at all and are created with an explicit `--id` from `create-task.sh <repo> <epicGate> suite`
and `… review`, so their ids read `0.1` and `0.2` under this gate.
The epic's own pull request aims at `main` — that is derived, not written, and it is the one merge in the tower
that reaches `main`. Then three edges: the suite blocks the gate, the review blocks the gate, the review waits
on the suite. Builds the tower and does no work in it.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/.claude/agents/plan-epic.md "Source — .claude/agents/plan-epic.md"
    }
    planFeature = planner "plan-feature" {
      summary "An epic into complete user-facing flows, each with its own branch, suite and review."
      description """
Epic → feature-sized features (complete user-facing flows). Each gets its own `feature/<slug>`, written onto
its gate and nowhere else. Its gate's id comes from `create-gate.sh <repo> <epicGateId>`, which copies the
epic's uuid and numbers the next feature; its `feature-test` and `feature-review` tickets carry no metadata and
are created with an explicit `--id` from `create-task.sh <repo> <gate> suite|review`. Where a feature's pull
request merges is never written here — the loop takes this gate's uuid, names the epic gate of that tower, and
reads its `branch`; a feature whose id names no epic gate is not aimed at `main`, it is refused
(`ERROR reason=no-epic-gate`). **No merge
ticket at this tier**: a feature has one branch and its tasks are the commits onto it. Four edges per feature —
the suite and the review each blocking the gate, the review waiting on the suite, and this feature's gate
blocking the epic's `epic-e2e-test`, which is what holds the epic tier off the frontier until every feature has
merged.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/.claude/agents/plan-feature.md "Source — .claude/agents/plan-feature.md"
    }
    planTask = planner "plan-task" {
      summary "One feature into its tasks, chained one to the next, every one blocking the feature's gate."
      description """
One feature → its tasks, **labelled at creation** with `bd create -l <label>`, each one created with an
explicit `--id` from `create-task.sh <repo> <featureGate>`, run once per task in chain order, and **no metadata
at all** — not even a `branch`: the loop reads the uuid off that id and takes both branches off the two gates it
names (a plan agent never invents a branch below its own
tier, and refuses with `ERROR reason=metadata-missing` when the feature's gate carries no `branch` of its own).
Before it writes each ticket it reads that kind's section-by-section template — `engineer.md` for a
`backend-logic` ticket, `frontend.md` for `frontend-ui`, `tester-unit.md` for `unit-tests` — three files under
`.claude/shared/plan-tasks/` in the **factory checkout**, and if its draft disagrees with the template the
template wins. The checkout is one of its working directories; its cwd stays in the tower, where no template
exists, because cwd is what decides which beads database `bd` reads.
Each task gets **two** edges —
onto the feature's gate, making it part of that tier, and onto the single task before it, ordering the build —
in one linked line, never a fan, and the tail hands to the `feature-test` ticket. Refuses outright without a
feature in the prompt: `ERROR reason=no-parent-feature`. Every ticket verified with a `bd show --json` round
trip, its `dependencies` among the fields checked.
"""
      link https://github.com/Londelo/spider-factory/blob/main/.claude/agents/plan-task.md "Source — .claude/agents/plan-task.md"
    }
  }

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE TOWER — a worked example. The machine above is verified against source;
  // this tower is an INVENTED example (a record box) used so the walkthroughs can
  // name real labels, branches and log lines.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  tower = layer "The tower — worked example: one epic, one feature" {
    summary "A worked example: one epic, one feature, nine tickets named by what they are."
    epic = gate "Epic gate" {
      summary "The epic gate — the what; closed once after the epic review, by a person or by the lead agent."
      description """
**What it is:** the epic gate — the *what*; closed once, after `epic-review`, by a person, or by the lead agent
when `SF_AGENT_EPIC_GATES=1`.

```
id:       box-epic-7f3a9c-0          ← the kind word says epic, the hierarchy 0 says top of the tower
type:     gate                       (custom type: bd config set types.custom \"gate\")
labels:   none — a gate carries zero labels, so no label can ever dispatch anything to it
branch:   epic/record-box            ← plan-epic's key, and the only key this row carries
pr:       https://github.com/…/pull/1 ← written by the LOOP, by ensureEpicGatePr
waits on: the epic review ticket      — an explicit blocked-by edge onto the gate itself
```

Two things about this row are **derived and never stored**. Its pull request aims at `main`, because the gate
its own id names is itself — `prTarget` is `main` exactly when the owner is the epic gate. And it is the
**epic** tier because its id says so: the kind word between the repo and the uuid is `epic`, which is the only
way the system knows a tier — no edge is walked to work it out.

Every ticket of the tier blocks this gate — that set of blockers **is** the tier, and it is the list the lead
agent reads when it asks whether the epic is proved. What holds the *next* tier off the frontier is an explicit
edge onto the gate itself, and the gate stays open until someone verifies it — so the tower stalls here until a
person, or a lead agent with its tier flag on, closes it. `bd ready` never shows a gate: it is structurally
invisible, so there is nothing to exclude, and the loop claims it by status instead.
"""
      epicE2e = ticket "epic-e2e-test" {
        summary "Journeys across the whole app in a real browser, once every feature gate has closed."
        description """
```
title:    journeys across the whole app in a real browser
id:       box-task-7f3a9c-0.1      ← create-task.sh printed it: the epic's suite is always .1
label:    epic-e2e-test        → AGENT → tester-e2e
metadata: none                     ← the loop reads the uuid off this id and lands on the epic gate for both branches
depends:  blocked-by every feature gate — one edge per feature, written by plan-feature
blocks:   the epic gate (its tier) and the epic review ticket
skill:    framework-playwright
```
**Brief:** state handoffs across the app, session continuity, one success and one failure journey through the
flow. Never re-test the micro tier's edges or the composition tier's wiring. Nothing is assembled for it: the
feature branches are already merged into `epic/record-box`, each by the lead agent standing on its own gate,
which is exactly why this ticket waits on the feature gates and not on a merge row.
"""
      }
      epicReview = ticket "epic-review" {
        summary "The three questions over the whole epic, posted on the epic pull request."
        description """
```
title:    the three questions over the whole epic
id:       box-task-7f3a9c-0.2
label:    epic-review          → AGENT → review
metadata: none                 ← same: its id reaches the epic gate, and the epic gate is its own target
depends:  blocked-by the end-to-end ticket
blocks:   the epic gate (its tier)
```
**Brief:** map entry → exit, instrument with trace logs, run the tests against the instrumented code, post
findings as PR comments. Then the epic gate is verified and closed — by a person, or by the lead agent proving
the tier and merging `epic/record-box` into `main` through the forge.
"""
      }
    }

    featA = gate "Feature gate" {
      summary "One complete flow — a user writes a record and sees it in the box; five tasks, one line."
      description """
```
id:       box-feature-7f3a9c-1     ← the kind word says feature; 1 is this feature's number under the epic
type:     gate
labels:   none — a gate carries zero labels
branch:   feature/add-record           ← plan-feature's key, and the only key this row carries
pr:       https://github.com/…/pull/2  ← written by the Stop hook onto the ticket that first landed work
waits on: the feature review ticket
```

Again nothing here says where its pull request goes: the loop takes this id's uuid, names
`box-epic-7f3a9c-0`, reads `epic/record-box` off that epic gate, and uses it as the **`prTarget`**. Nor is there
any other record of it being a **feature** gate — the kind word in its own id is the whole of the tier test.
One complete user-facing flow: a user writes a record and sees it in the box. Its five tasks are **one linked
line, never a fan**, so the frontier can only ever hand out one task per feature and this branch is never
written by two workers at once. Every one of them blocks this gate, and so do the suite and the review — that
set of blockers is the tier the lead agent proves before it merges `feature/add-record` into `epic/record-box`
through the forge, which is what closing this gate means.
"""
      backendA = ticket "backend-logic" {
        summary "validateRecord and saveRecord as pure functions: the first task, and the only one claimable."
        description """
```
title:    validateRecord + saveRecord — pure functions
label:    backend-logic        → AGENT → engineer
category: backend-logic
id:       box-task-7f3a9c-1.3      ← create-task.sh printed it; a number is a place, not an order
metadata: none                     ← the loop reads both branches off the gates this id names
depends:  none — the first task, and the only ticket claimable when the run starts
blocks:   the feature gate (its tier) and the unit-tests ticket
```
**Requirements**
1. `validateRecord(input): Either<RecordValidationError, Record>` — pure, no mutation
2. `saveRecord(validated)` through the repository port only
3. reject: empty title, title > 120 chars, duplicate slug, missing body

**Files:** `src/records/validate-record.ts`, `src/records/types.ts`, `src/records/ports.ts`
**Done means:** the work committed on the branch, and your result commented on this ticket — the final message is
the live report, the ticket is what outlives the session, and the duty itself is the agent file's
`## Report On The Ticket`, not this description's. The Stop hook files it, not you.
"""
      }
      unitA = ticket "unit-tests" {
        summary "Exhaustive coverage of validateRecord: every branch, every boundary, every error."
        description """
```
title:    exhaustive coverage for validateRecord
label:    unit-tests           → AGENT → tester-unit
category: unit-tests
id:       box-task-7f3a9c-1.4
metadata: none
depends:  blocked-by the back-end ticket — the ticket it tests, its one and only predecessor
blocks:   the feature gate (its tier) and the front-end ticket
skill:    framework-jest
```
**Cover:** every branch, every boundary (120/121 chars), empty and null input, each error it throws, the empty
body.
**Owned elsewhere:** composition wiring (feature tier), components (never here), journeys (epic tier).
"""
      }
      frontendA = ticket "frontend-ui" {
        summary "RecordForm and RecordCard against proved functions; its close unblocks the suite."
        description """
```
title:    RecordForm + RecordCard against proved functions
label:    frontend-ui          → AGENT → frontend
category: frontend-ux          ← the CATEGORY; the LABEL is frontend-ui
id:       box-task-7f3a9c-1.5
metadata: none
depends:  blocked-by the unit tests ticket
blocks:   the feature gate (its tier) and the feature's suite — the tail of the chain hands to it
```
**Brief:** the form, validation feedback surfacing each `RecordValidationError`, the card in the box. Layout and
styling only — the logic is already proved by the micro tier. This ticket landing is what unblocks `feature-test`.
"""
      }
      featureTestA = ticket "feature-test" {
        summary "The add-a-record pieces proved wired, once every task has landed on the branch."
        description """
```
title:    the add-record pieces are wired
label:    feature-test         → AGENT → tester-feature
category: feature-test
id:       box-task-7f3a9c-1.1      ← always .1: a lower number than the tasks it waits on
metadata: none
depends:  blocked-by the front-end ticket — the LAST task; nothing is proved against half a feature
blocks:   the feature gate (its tier) and the feature's review ticket
skill:    framework-playwright
```
**Cover:** the composed flow saves a record; each encapsulated error surfaces to the user.
"""
      }
      featureReviewA = ticket "feature-review" {
        summary "The three questions over feature/add-record; then the human verifies and closes the gate."
        description """
```
title:    the three questions over feature/add-record
label:    feature-review       → AGENT → review
category: review
id:       box-task-7f3a9c-1.2
metadata: none
depends:  blocked-by the feature's suite — no review before the suite
blocks:   the feature gate (its tier)
```
**Brief:** three questions from first principles, findings as PR comments. Then the feature gate is verified and
closed — by a person, or by the lead agent proving the tier and merging `feature/add-record` into
`epic/record-box` with `gh pr merge` / `glab mr merge` on that PR.
"""
      }
    }
  }

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // THE ALARMS — every reason string in the engine and the Stop hook, and what
  // happens to the ticket when it fires. All of them are walked in views.c4.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  alarms = layer "Alarms — every reason= in the code" {
    summary "Every reason the engine and the Stop hook can log, and what happens to the ticket."

    startupAlarms = layer "raised before the first tick — the run never starts" {
      summary "The alarms that refuse the run before its first tick."
      toolsMissing = alarm "startup: <tool> not found on PATH" {
        #fatal
        summary "A tool the loop needs is not on PATH, so the run exits before its first tick."
        description """
`requireToolsOnPath` wants `bd jq git` plus the first word of `SF_AGENT_COMMAND`, and `exit 1`s on the first
one missing. Note the Stop hook has its own list — `bd jq git sf` — shorter because it never spawns a session,
and carrying `sf` because that is the command it commits with.
"""
      }
      knobNotNumeric = alarm "startup: SF_<VAR>=<x> is not a number" {
        #fatal
        summary "A numeric knob was set to something that is not a number, so the run refuses to start."
        description """
`SF_MAX_PARALLEL`, `SF_POLL_S` or `SF_MAX_TICKETS` set to anything but digits. There is no defaulting silently:
a knob typed wrong would otherwise change how many workers run unattended.
"""
      }
      gateFlagNotASwitch = alarm "startup: SF_AGENT_<TIER>_GATES=<x> is not a switch" {
        #fatal
        summary "A gate tier switch is neither 0 nor 1, so the run refuses to guess who closes that tier's gate."
        description """
`SF_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES` and `SF_AGENT_EPIC_GATES` are switches, not numbers: `0` means a person closes that
tier's gate, `1` means the lead agent does. Anything else — `true`, `yes`, `2` — prints the flag with both
meanings and exits 1. Checked after the numeric knobs, before the poll interval; unset is `0`, so this is always
a value somebody typed. There is no defaulting here either: a value read loosely would silently hand a tier's
merges to a machine, or silently withhold them from it.
"""
      }
      pollUnusable = alarm "startup: SF_POLL_S=<x> is unusable — it must divide 60" {
        #fatal
        summary "The poll interval does not divide 60, so the waiting-line cadence would be a lie."
        description """
The permitted list is printed: `1 2 3 4 5 6 10 12 15 20 30 60`. It must divide 60 because the waiting lines are
rate-limited at one per minute — `60 / SF_POLL_S` ticks — and a poll that does not divide the minute makes that
cadence a lie.
"""
      }
      noBeadsDb = alarm "startup: no beads database found from $PWD" {
        #fatal
        summary "This directory answers no ticket list, so nothing can be done here."
        description """
`bd list --limit 1 --json` must answer an **array**. It is a real probe of \"can we do anything here\", not a
check for a `.beads` folder — `bd` walks **up** from the cwd and ignores `BEADS_DIR`, so a database in the
environment does not save a wrong directory. `sf` runs the same probe before it even starts the engine.
"""
      }
      logDirUnwritable = alarm "startup: cannot create <dir>" {
        #fatal
        summary "The run's log directory cannot be created, so the run could never raise an alarm."
        description """
`mkdir -p $SF_LOG_ROOT/<timestamp>` failed. Fatal on purpose: a run that cannot write its logs cannot raise an
alarm, and a silent dispatcher is the one thing this design refuses to be.
"""
      }
      labelsStarved = alarm "not fatal: SF_ALLOWED_LABELS names nothing the map holds" {
        #fatal
        summary "The allowed labels name nothing the map holds: not fatal, but the tower never moves."
        description """
The one startup mistake that does **not** stop the run. A typo'd label narrows the allowed set to something no
ticket carries, so every claim matches zero keys, every claim is `label-ambiguous`, and the tower sits still
while the waiting lines keep arriving. Compare the files by hand: `bd list --json` labels against the map.
"""
      }
    }

    tickAlarms = layer "raised by the tick" {
      summary "Everything the tick can refuse, and whether the ticket stays claimed."
      bdFailure = alarm "ERROR reason=bd-failure exit=N" {
        summary "The claim failed or answered without an id; the tick returns and the next one tries again."
        description """
Two forms, one reason. `bd ready --claim` exited non-zero and the last line of `$logDir/bd-stderr` is quoted in
`msg=`; or it exited `0` and handed back no `id`, with the first 60 characters of the payload in `msg=`. The tick
returns; there is no claim to keep or return, so the next tick tries again. An **empty** answer is not this
alarm at all — that is a waiting line.
"""
      }
      labelAmbiguous = alarm "ERROR reason=label-ambiguous" {
        #leftClaimed
        summary "Zero or two dispatching labels on the claim; the loop never guesses a worker."
        description """
Zero **or two** dispatching labels on a claim: `matched=\"…\" labels=\"…\"`. The dispatcher never guesses a
specialist. The ticket is LEFT CLAIMED so its 5-minute lease parks it off the frontier — otherwise the loop
would spin on the same unworkable ticket every tick — and the errors file is the alarm.

The commonest cause is a ticket labelled with its **category** instead of its label (`frontend-ux` for
`frontend-ui`), which matches nothing and reads identically to a ticket with no labels at all.
"""
      }
      noEpicGate = alarm "ERROR reason=no-epic-gate ticket=<id>" {
        #leftClaimed
        summary "The ticket's id reaches no gate: it belongs to no tower. The id is in the line."
        description """
Three forms, one reason, and the ticket's own id on every one of them — that is the point of the alarm, so a
person can find the row. The loop derives a ticket's whole git world off its id, and that only reaches a gate
when the id is shaped like a tower's, reading `<repo>-<kind>-<uuid>-<hierarchy>`:

| form | when |
|---|---|
| (no `step=`) | the id does not read that grammar at all — a `bd create` with no `--id` mints exactly this |
| `step=not-below-a-gate` | it reads, and calls itself a `task`, but its hierarchy carries no gate number — a task hangs below a gate or nowhere |
| `gate=<the epic id it names>` | the epic gate its uuid names is a real ticket that is **not** a gate |

Nothing was dispatched and the ticket is LEFT CLAIMED, so its five-minute lease parks it off the frontier
instead of the loop spinning on it. It is a plan bug, and it stays a plan bug where you can see it: the loop
never invents a gate and never walks a graph looking for one.
"""
      }
      ticketGateUnworkable = alarm "ERROR reason=gate-unworkable ticket=<id>" {
        #leftClaimed
        summary "The ticket's id reaches its gates, but one of them carries no branch."
        description """
Two forms, and the second carries `gate=`. `owner-carries-no-branch`: the gate this ticket's work lands on —
its feature gate, or the epic gate for an epic-tier ticket — has no `branch` of its own, so there is no folder
to work in and nothing to push. `epic-carries-no-branch`: the work has a home but the epic gate above it has no
`branch`, so the pull request has nothing to aim at.

Left claimed, same reason as the label alarm. The gate is the only place a branch is ever written, so a gate
with no `branch` is a plan bug, and the loop never guesses one to cover it.
"""
      }
      epicPrFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=epic-pr-failed step=…" {
        #unclaimed
        summary "The epic branch or its pull request could not be published; seven steps say where."
        description """
The epic branch or its PR could not be published — and every feature PR below it aims at that branch, so nothing
beneath the epic has a pull-request target until this succeeds. Seven steps name exactly where it died:

| step | when |
|---|---|
| `gate-branch` | the epic gate carries no `branch` metadata of its own, so nothing beneath it has a pull-request target |
| `forge` | `detectForge` settled on nothing |
| `missing-tool` | the settled forge's CLI (`gh`/`glab`) is not installed |
| `seed` | the branch's own first commit could not be written — `git commit-tree` needs a `user.name` and a `user.email` |
| `push` | the epic branch could not be published off `main` |
| `pr` | no URL matching `/(pull\|merge_requests)/[0-9]+` came back |
| `annotate` | `bd update --set-metadata pr=` refused |

Nothing is walked to find the epic: the claim's id was already read into `$gateId` by `resolveTicketBranches`,
two lines earlier, and that is the gate this function is handed. So `gate-branch` is
the only plan-bug step here, and it is the same bug `gate-unworkable` raises one step earlier — a gate nobody
gave a branch.

A forge will not open a pull request whose head and base are the same commit, so the branch is born with one
commit of its own, `git commit-tree` off `main` — it never touches the tower's working tree and never moves its
`HEAD`. A branch already there is read first and left alone unless it sits on `main`'s own commit.

Every one of the seven releases the claim with `bd unclaim` and the next tick tries again — the ticket is
innocent whatever the step, because the failure is the machine's or the gate's and never the ticket's. No worker
is ever spawned into a branch that does not exist, because the claim is released before the spawn. The one place
the epic gate's own branch appears is `gateBranch=` on the line — never `branch=`, which belongs to the claim, and
the two differ.
"""
      }
      worktreeFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=worktree-failed step=…" {
        #leftClaimed
        summary "The branch's folder could not be built, so no worker is spawned into it; four steps say where."
        description """
`ensureWorktree` could not build the folder, so no worker is spawned into it. Left claimed, like a mislabelled
ticket: a worker spawned into a missing folder burns a slot on nothing. It self-heals what it can first — a stale
`git worktree` registration is pruned, and a folder left by an interrupted build is moved aside rather than
overwritten — and only then refuses.

| step | when |
|---|---|
| `no-start` | none of `origin/<branch>`, `origin/<prTarget>`, `<prTarget>`, `HEAD` resolves to a commit |
| `stray-folder` | the branch's folder exists, is not a registered worktree, and could not be renamed aside |
| `gate` | the same failure on a **gate** dispatch — the line carries `gate=<id>`, and the gate is put back to `open` rather than left claimed, so the next tick may try it again |
| *(no step)* | `git worktree add` itself failed against the starting commit it did find |
"""
      }
      branchHasUnpushed = alarm "ERROR reason=branch-has-unpushed-commits" {
        #leftClaimed
        summary "The local branch holds commits no remote has seen; the loop refuses to reset a branch that does."
        description """
`git rev-list --count <branch> --not --remotes=origin` came back above zero: somebody's work sits on this local
branch and has never reached a remote. Rebuilding the folder means `git worktree add -B`, which **resets the
branch to the starting commit** — so the loop refuses instead of deleting a worker's commits. Left claimed.

The recovery is written into the line itself: `git reflog` to find them, or push them, then run the loop again.
This is the only worktree alarm that says what to do, because it is the only one where proceeding costs work
rather than time.
"""
      }
      folderMovedAside = alarm "ERROR reason=worktree-folder-moved-aside" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "Not a refusal: an interrupted build's files were kept under a .stale-<epoch> name and the build went ahead."
        description """
The branch's folder existed, was not a registered worktree, and held files — the shape a build leaves when it
dies between creating the folder and registering it. `git worktree add` into a non-empty folder fails outright,
so the folder is renamed to `<path>.stale-<epoch>` and the branch is built fresh. The line is written to `errors`
and the worker **is** spawned: nothing was deleted, and the kept folder is named on the line.
"""
      }
      workerNotDispatched = alarm "ERROR reason=worker-not-dispatched" {
        #unclaimed
        summary "The spawn came back failed; the one alarm that puts the ticket straight back."
        description """
`$SF_AGENT_LAUNCH_COMMAND --bg` came back non-zero; the last line of that worker's `spawn-<ticket>` file is quoted
in `msg=`. An agent name with no file behind it lands here, and so does a session that could not discover
`.claude/agents/` at all — which is what the launch command's `--add-dir <factory root>` exists to prevent.
`bd unclaim` — unlike every other tick alarm, this one puts the ticket **straight back**, because nothing was
ever spawned to work it and the next tick may well succeed.

On a **gate** dispatch the line carries `gate=<id>` instead of a ticket, and the put-back is `moveGateTo <gate>
open in_progress` — the gate returns to `open`, off the ready list no longer, so the next tick may reach it
again.
"""
      }
      gateClaimLost = alarm "ERROR reason=gate-claim-lost gate=<id>" {
        #idle
        summary "The gate was no longer open the moment the loop reached for it; no lead agent was spawned."
        description """
The gate claim is `bd update <gate> --status in_progress --if-status open`, and `--if-status` is what makes it
safe: if the gate was no longer `open` the instant the loop reached for it — another loop got there first, or a
person closed it by hand — the write fails, the alarm fires, and **no lead agent is spawned**. Nothing is
spawned into a gate someone else already holds, and nothing is closed twice.

This is the only conditional write anywhere in the system, and it is also what takes a gate off the ready list
while its lead agent works: a gate never appears in `bd ready`, so status is the only handle on it. If that lead
agent dies, **nothing parks the gate** — no hook fires when a session dies, so it stays `in_progress`, out of the
ready-gate list for the same reason, and looks exactly like a lead agent still working. The overseer's check for a
worker that died quietly is the only thing that finds one, and all it can do is hand a person
`bd update <gate> --status failed-agent` to run.
"""
      }
    }

    stopAlarms = layer "raised by the Stop hook — fail-closed, in code order" {
      summary "Everything the Stop hook refuses, in code order: fail-closed, ticket untouched."
      claimMalformedHook = alarm "ERROR reason=claim-malformed" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The session's claims line carries no ticket or no run, so the hook cannot tell what it stands for."
        description """
The hook finds this session's line in the run's `claims` file and reads its `key=value` fields off it. A line
with no `ticket=` or no `run=` is unreadable — no ticket, no run directory — so the hook stops
with the ticket still `in_progress`. It is the first refusal in the file, and only a session the loop did
dispatch can reach it: a session with **no** claims line exits quietly one line earlier, which is how every
session the loop never started leaves.
"""
      }
      noContextHook = alarm "ERROR reason=no-context worktree=<dir>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "A worker stopped in a loop worktree with no dispatch context file and no branch or target to fall back on."
        description """
The claims line names the worktree; the dispatch context file beside it names `branch`, `pr_target` and `kind`.
This fires when that file cannot be read **and** the claims line carries no `branch=` or no `target=` — with
either one the hook carries on off the claims line alone. Nothing landed, nothing was committed, and the ticket
stays claimed.
"""
      }
      missingToolHook = alarm "ERROR reason=missing-tool tool=<t>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "bd, jq, git or sf is missing; checked before any of the hook's own work, once the session is known."
        description """
`bd`, `jq`, `git` or `sf` missing — checked before any of the work, but after the claims line has been matched,
so a session the loop never spawned still exits on line one. `sf` is there because the hook commits
through it, and the CLI is installed before any worker ever runs.
"""
      }
      noWorktreeHook = alarm "ERROR reason=no-worktree" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The claims line names no worktree that exists, and the hook will not guess one off the hook input's cwd."
        description """
The worktree comes only from the `worktree=` field of this session's claims line. If that is empty or the
directory is not there, the hook refuses — it will not take the hook input's own `.cwd` instead. Guessing here
means committing whatever happens to sit in some other project's tree.
"""
      }
      ticketNotInWork = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-not-in-work state=<s>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The ticket is not in progress; if it is closed, this is a second Stop and the hook leaves."
        description """
The ticket is not `in_progress`. Two readings: state `closed` means this is a second Stop in a session that
already shipped, and the hook leaves **silently** rather than committing onto a shared branch a second time;
anything else (`not-found`, `open`) is an alarm.
"""
      }
      noBranchHook = alarm "ERROR reason=no-branch" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The dispatch record carries no branch, so the hook refuses to file work anywhere."
        description """
Neither the session's claims line (`branch=`) nor that worktree's dispatch context file (`branch=`) names a branch,
so there is no branch to file on — the loop never got as far as deriving one off the gate the ticket's id names, and
that gate carries no `branch` key. The spawn is nowhere in this: it sets no environment, so the value the hook is
missing is one it would have exported for itself. The check is `requireDispatchRecord`, and its `msg=` names the
dispatch record and the gate that should have carried the key.
"""
      }
      noPrTarget = alarm "ERROR reason=no-pr-target" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The dispatch record carries no pull-request target, so the hook refuses to aim blind."
        description """
The loop derived that target off the ticket's epic gate — the gate its uuid names, `main` when the owner is the epic
gate itself — and wrote it into the dispatch record: the claims line's `target=`, with the context file's
`pr_target=` as the fallback. Neither carried it, so the hook would be aiming blind, and it refuses to. The spawn set
no environment, so nothing ever "was not on the spawn": the record is the only place this value was ever written.
"""
      }
      wrongBranch = alarm "ERROR reason=wrong-branch want=<b> got=<d>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The worker changed branch anyway, so the hook will not file its work under this ticket."
        description """
The worker was told never to change branches; if it did anyway, this is the last place that can refuse to file
its work under the wrong ticket's name. `git branch --show-current` must equal the branch off the dispatch record
exactly — a detached tree reads as `got=\"detached\"`.
"""
      }
      noTitle = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-has-no-title" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The commit message is the ticket title, and a titleless ticket cannot be filed."
        description """
The commit message IS the ticket title (`bd show --json --long | .title`), so a ticket with no title cannot be
filed — the commit would be anonymous on a shared branch.
"""
      }
      commitFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=commit-failed" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "Committing the tree failed — a genuinely empty tree is allowed, this is not one."
        description """
`sf commit \"<title>

Ticket: <id>\"` run inside the worktree — which is `git add -A`, `git commit -m \"<agent>:<branch>:<that
message>\"`, `git push -u origin <branch>`, all of it `CLI/src/commit.sh`. A review or test
ticket may legitimately land nothing (`git diff --cached --quiet` before it skips the commit entirely and the hook
carries on to push, PR and close), so this is a genuine commit failure, not an empty tree: it fires when the index
is **still dirty** after `sf commit` returned.
"""
      }
      pushFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=push-failed" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "Pushing the branch to the remote failed; its output is quoted on the line."
        description """
`git push -u origin <branch>` failed, the branch being the one off this session's dispatch record; the first 160
characters of its output are the `msg=`.
"""
      }
      forgeUnknown = alarm "ERROR reason=forge-unknown remote=<url>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "Nothing settled which forge this remote is, and the hook refuses to guess."
        description """
All three settle steps came up empty — the remote name lied, it publishes neither `refs/pull/` nor
`refs/merge-requests/`, and the machine holds both `gh` and `glab`, or neither. The hook refuses to guess a forge.
Reached **after** the commit and the push, because the hook only needs the forge to open a PR.
"""
      }
      forgeToolMissing = alarm "ERROR reason=missing-tool tool=gh|glab forge=<f>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The forge is known, but its command line tool is not installed."
        description """
The forge settled, its CLI is not installed. Same reason as the up-front check, different `tool=` and with
`forge=` appended — which is how you tell the two apart in the errors file.
"""
      }
      prNotFoundGate = alarm "ERROR reason=pr-not-found forge=<f>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The lead agent stopped on a gate whose branch has no pull request at all — the gate path's own refusal."
        description """
Reached only when the claim's `kind` is `gate`. The gate path never opens a pull request — the lead agent was sent
to **merge** one, so the hook only looks the branch's PR up at any state, `--state all` on GitHub and `--all` on
GitLab, against that branch and its `prTarget`. Nothing matches: there was nothing for the lead agent to merge,
and the lead agent's session ended anyway. The gate stays claimed and the tier behind it waits.

This is the reason that does not exist on the task path, and the two are not the same failure: `pr-not-created`
means the hook could not raise a pull request, this means one was never there to merge. Only two things ever open
one — the loop, for the **epic** branch, the first time it claims anything under that epic (`epic-published`),
and the Stop hook, for a **feature** branch, the first time work lands on it. So a feature gate reaching this
line means no worker's work ever landed on that branch and its tier was closed some other way, by a hand and not
by the hook.
"""
      }
      prNotCreated = alarm "ERROR reason=pr-not-created forge=<f>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "Nothing came back that looks like a pull request url."
        description """
Nothing came back that looks like a PR url. Either the list of the branch's existing PRs and the create both
failed, or the CLI printed an error quoting some other URL — only `/(pull|merge_requests)/<digits>` counts.
"""
      }
      ticketNotAnnotated = alarm "ERROR reason=ticket-not-annotated pr=<url>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "Writing the pull request url onto the ticket failed, so the hook will not close it."
        description """
`bd update --set-metadata pr=<url>` failed. The work is pushed but the ticket cannot say where it went, so the
hook stops rather than closing a ticket whose PR is unfindable. (`--set-metadata` **merges**, so this never
disturbs the plan's `branch` key.)
"""
      }
      closeFailed = alarm "ERROR reason=close-failed pr=<url>" {
        #staysClaimed
        summary "The final close failed: the work is out there with no record in the tower."
        description """
`bd close <ticket> --session <session_id> --reason \"worker session … finished; PR …\"` failed. The last step, and
the only one where everything else already succeeded — the commit, the push and the PR are all out there with no
record in the tower. `bd` refuses to close a ticket whose assignee is not the actor, and the dispatcher claimed
this one, so the hook deliberately passes the real `session_id` rather than overriding the actor.
"""
      }
    }

  }

  waiting = layer "Waiting lines — nothing failed, nothing ran (one a minute per reason)" {
    summary "The quiet ticks: nothing failed, nothing ran, one line a minute per reason."
    frontierEmpty = waitingLine "reason=frontier_empty" {
      #idle
      summary "Nothing waits anywhere: the tower is finished or unplanned. The calm one."
      description """
The claim came back empty and the stall probe found **no** off-frontier work: the tower is finished, or nobody has
planned anything yet. This is the calm one.
"""
    }
    towerStalled = waitingLine "reason=tower_stalled waiting=N" {
      #idle
      summary "Work exists but a blocker holds it off the frontier — usually a gate whose tier is still a person's."
      description """
The other half of an empty frontier, and the two need different alarms: work tickets **do** exist
(`bd list --status open,blocked --limit 0` counted N of them) but an active blocker holds them off `bd ready` —
an open gate two levels below, most often. That is the expected shape of a run: a gate is a checkpoint, and while
its tier's flag is `0` nothing dispatches it, so everything behind it waits for a person. `bd list` hides closed
tickets and gates by default, so N counts work tickets only. A broken tower must never read as a calm one.
"""
    }
    gateUnworkable = waitingLine "reason=gate_unworkable gate=<id> step=<why>" {
      #idle
      summary "A ready gate's id reaches no usable branch and target, so no lead agent can be placed or aimed."
      description """
A gate reached the frontier and `resolveTicketBranches` refused its id. `step=owner-carries-no-branch` — the gate
carries no `branch` of its own, so nowhere to put the tier's work; `epic-carries-no-branch` — the epic gate its
uuid names carries none, so its pull request has nothing to aim at; `not-a-gate` — the epic gate its uuid names
is a row that is not a gate; `malformed` — the id does not read
`<repo>-<epic|-feature>-<uuid>-<number>` at all. Same rule as the ticket
path, same refusal: the loop never invents a branch and never guesses a target. The consequence is quieter here —
with no lead agent spawned the gate is never claimed, so it stays `open`, shows up ready again next tick, and the
tier behind it simply waits. An alarm would fire every tick forever; this is a line, rate-limited to one a minute.
"""
    }
    maxParallel = waitingLine "reason=max_parallel in_progress=N" {
      #idle
      summary "The cap is reached and tickets are in progress, so this tick claims nothing."
      description """
`SF_MAX_PARALLEL` of this tower's background sessions report themselves busy and N tickets are in progress — the
workers are working, so the tick simply does not claim. A session that has finished reports idle and is not one of
them, so it never contributes to reaching the cap.
"""
    }
    maxParallelStalled = waitingLine "reason=max_parallel_stalled in_progress=0" {
      #idle
      summary "At capacity with nothing in progress: the counted workers are ghosts."
      description """
At capacity with **nothing in progress**: the slots are ghosts — sessions counted busy whose tickets are gone. The
count comes fresh from `$SF_AGENT_COMMAND agents --json` every tick precisely so this state is visible rather than silent.
"""
    }
  }

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // The depends-on edges, as edges of the model rather than of one walkthrough.
  // A static view draws only these; a dynamic view draws only the edges its own
  // steps create — so the graph view needs them here. The edges that make a TIER
  // are absent, every one of them: each row blocks its own gate, and a gate is
  // drawn as the box around its tier's rows, so the engine refuses those edges as
  // parent-child. The gate's own panel is the list of its blockers.
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  tower.featA.backendA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.unitA "then its tests"
  tower.featA.unitA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.frontendA "then the interface"
  tower.featA.frontendA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.featureTestA "then the suite"
  tower.featA.featureTestA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.featureReviewA "then the review"

  tower.featA -[blocks]-> tower.epic.epicE2e "no journey over an unmerged flow"
  tower.epic.epicE2e -[blocks]-> tower.epic.epicReview "the journeys, then the review"
}
